<node id="hierba:blighia-sapida-k-d-koenig-akee-apples"><data key="n_label">Akee Apples, Seso Vegetal, Ackee</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Cupania sapida J. Voigt</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">AKEE APPLES, SESO VEGETAL, ACKEE</data><data key="n_page">7</data><data key="n_scientific">Blighia sapida K. D. Koenig</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_downsides">Used as a fish poison. Contains the potentially useful but toxic hypoglycemic agents, cyclopropanoid amino acids, hypoglycin A, and hypoglycin B. Akee poisoning has been reported to be fatal in 2 hours. The bitter reddish raphe should be picked out as the fruit opens on the tree. Damaged, unripe, or fallen fruits should not be eaten. Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, a quiescent period with drowsiness and sleep, followed 3 or 4 hours later with intense vomiting, and lastly, convulsions, coma, and death.</data></node>
