<node id="hierba:hedeoma-pulegioides-american-pennyroyal"><data key="n_label">American Pennyroyal</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Melissa pulegioides L.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">AMERICAN PENNYROYAL</data><data key="n_page">25</data><data key="n_scientific">Hedeoma pulegioides (L.) Pers.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+ (down from CRC)</data><data key="n_dosages">Should not be taken (APA).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2b. Emmenagogue and uterotonic (AHP). In Canada, only allowed in food or beverage if pulegone-free (AHP). Used as an emmenagogue and abortifacient. Pennyroyal oil is toxic and potentially fatal if ingested. Symptoms include abdominal cramps, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, rash, dizziness, and alternating lethargy and agitation, leading to kidney failure, massive hepatic necrosis, and disseminated intravascular coagulation. Serious toxicity with the tea has not been reported. CNS symptoms from ingesting pennyroyal-containing preparations include lethargy, agitation, dizziness, sometimes leading to seizures and auditory and visual hallucinations. GI effects include nausea, vomiting, burning in the throat, abdominal pain, and diarrhea (AEH1).</data></node>
