<node id="hierba:andira-araroba-aguan-araroba"><data key="n_label">Araroba</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">ARAROBA</data><data key="n_page">36</data><data key="n_scientific">Andira araroba Aguan</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">One-half grain (GMH).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not in AHP; APA; KOM. Caution: easily absorbed dermally and potentially nephrotoxic (EFS); 200 mg internally can induce diarrhea, nausea, and nephrosis. Powder is a respiratory irritant (CRC); sawdust muco- and occuloirritant (CRC). Severely irritant to the skin and mucous membranes. As little as 100 mg administered externally can lead to diarrhea, nephritis, and vomiting (PH2).</data></node>
