<node id="hierba:gypsophila-paniculata-l-baby-s-breath"><data key="n_label">Baby'S Breath</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BABY'S BREATH</data><data key="n_page">49</data><data key="n_scientific">Gypsophila paniculata L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">The dosage mentioned by Blumenthal et al., 30–150 mg dry root or 3–15 mg saponin, is very different than that of Gruenwald, 1.5 g/day (KOM; PHR).</data><data key="n_downsides">Commission E reports no contraindications, adverse effects, or interactions except for gastric irritation (AEH). Blumenthal et al. reports that it irritates the mucous membranes. Unlike Blumenthal et al., Gruenwald et al. treats the more dangerous Gypsophila under the slightly less dangerous Saponaria, both wellendowed with saponins.</data></node>
<node id="act:mucoirritant"><data key="n_label">Mucoirritant</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 7 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:aegle-marmelos-bael"><data key="n_label">Bael, Bengal Quince</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Belou marmelos (L.) A. Lyons, Crateva marmelos L.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BAEL, BENGAL QUINCE</data><data key="n_page">49</data><data key="n_scientific">Aegle marmelos (L.) Correa</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">2–12 g powdered fruit (KAP); 12–20 ml infusion (KAP); 28–56 ml decoction (KAP).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM).“Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) (but PH2 designates no specific quantified dosage! JAD). Tannin may constipate or cause dyspepsia (PH2).</data></node>
