<node id="hierba:betula-spp-birch"><data key="n_label">Birch</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BIRCH</data><data key="n_page">76</data><data key="n_scientific">Betula spp.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">1–2 tbsp chopped leaf/cup water, several ×/day (APA); 2–3 g several ×/day (KOM; PIP); 12 g/day (SHT).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP). Contraindications reported as edema due to cardiac or renal insufficiency (AEH). Not for use in edema in patients with cardiac or nephrotic problems. (PH2). None reported (PIP). “Health hazards not known with proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). Warning: EO toxic; easily absorbed through the skin (FAD).</data></node>
<node id="act:saluretic"><data key="n_label">Saluretic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 7 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:aristolochia-clematitis-l-birthwort"><data key="n_label">Birthwort</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BIRTHWORT</data><data key="n_page">77</data><data key="n_scientific">Aristolochia clematitis L.</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_dosages">Don’t take it.</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2b, 3 (AHP). Contraindicated in pregnancy (PH2). Outlawed in U.S. because of aristolochic acid.</data></node>
<node id="act:nephrotoxic"><data key="n_label">Nephrotoxic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 8 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:ammi-visnaga-bishops-weed"><data key="n_label">Bishop’S Weed</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Daucus visnaga L.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BISHOP’S WEED</data><data key="n_page">77</data><data key="n_scientific">Ammi visnaga (L.) Lamarck</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">20 mg pyrones, calculated as khellin (BIS); 0.5 g powdered fruit/cup water (1 tsp = 2.5 g) (BIS).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). Adverse effects may include dizziness, elevated hepatic transaminase, fatigue, insomnia, pseudoallergic reactions, reversible cholestatic jaundice, vertigo, and vomiting (PHR; SHT; WO2). Prolonged use or overdose may cause allergic symptoms, anorexia, constipation, elevated liver enzymes, headache, insomnia, nausea, queasiness, and vertigo (BIS; PHR). Phototoxic coumarins. Commission E approved Bishop’s Weed on March 13, 1986, but reversed itself as of April 15, 1994, condemning it because of excessive therapeutic risk and unproven efficacy (renal colic, spastic urinary tract disorders). “Ammi fruits may no longer be prescribed in Germany for this or any other indication” (SHT). Extracts and khellin and visnagin improve myocardial perfusion, increasing blood flow through the coronary vessels, making it useful for angina.</data></node>
