<node id="ind:lymphoma"><data key="n_label">Lymphoma</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 18 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:iberis-amara-l-bitter-candytuft"><data key="n_label">Bitter Candytuft</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Iberis coronaria D. Don.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BITTER CANDYTUFT</data><data key="n_page">78</data><data key="n_scientific">Iberis amara L.</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_dosages">Homeopathic doses only (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). Poisonings, not reported, could be caused by cucurbitacins and might exhibit colic, diarrhea, kidney irritation, and vomiting (PH2). No toxic cases documented (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="ind:stenocardia"><data key="n_label">Stenocardia</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 6 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:momordica-charantia-l-bitter-melon"><data key="n_label">Bitter Melon, Balsam Pear</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: M. muricata Willd.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BITTER MELON, BALSAM PEAR</data><data key="n_page">78</data><data key="n_scientific">Momordica charantia L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">1 (500 mg) capsule 3 ×/day with 150 mg extract containing 2.5% bitter principles (NH); one small melon per day (SKY); 50 ml fresh juice/day (SKY); 5 ml tincture 2–3 ×/day (SKY); 2–5 g leaf/liter water (MPG).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM; PHR). High doses may cause diarrhea and enteralgia (SKY); may potentiate hypoglycemic drugs (SKY); small children and patients with hypoglycemia should avoid (SKY). Seeds contain abortifacient compounds. This one is too new, at least in the First World, to have entered any of the Commission E books I am consulting for my herbal desk reference. Even TRAMIL, the Caribbean Commission E I call Commission T, does not recommend this frequent edible weed due to some toxic compounds it contains.</data><data key="n_extracts">At least three groups of hypoglycemic compounds (SKY). Alphaand beta-momocharin inhibit HIV in vitro (SKY). Various extracts active in vitro against Bacillus subtilis, Corynebacterium diptheriae, Escherichia coli, Neisseria spp., Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Salmonella, Sarcina lutea, Staphylococcus aureus, and Streptococcus spp., LD50 of fruit; 3 g/kg (TRA; ZUL). Charantin, diosgenin, and beta-sitosterol uterotonic (ZUL); but decoction of fruits and green leaves, without root, at 25,000 mg/kg produced no deaths in rats. Alphatrichosanthine and beta-momorchin in the seeds are abortifacient. Hydroethanolic fruit extracts, 1.75 g/dog orally for 20 days, lowered spermatogenesis. Aqueous extracts of leaves at 500 mg/kg orl rat was neither embryotoxic nor estrogenic. Leaf juice not topically allergic nor irritant (TRA).</data></node>
