<node id="ind:tenesmus"><data key="n_label">Tenesmus</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 23 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:quassia-amara-l-bitterwood"><data key="n_label">Bitterwood</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BITTERWOOD</data><data key="n_page">81</data><data key="n_scientific">Quassia amara L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">Quoting an old Merck recommendation, 1–2 g wood/day as decoction; average dose 0.5 g, 2–3 ×/day (AHP); 5 drops tincture 3 ×/day (MAD); 500 mg (HHB; PHR; PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2b (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). Contraindicated in pregnancy (PHR; PH2). Overdose is a gastroirritant, leading to nausea (AHP). Extended use may damage vision (PHR).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:alnus-glutinosa-black-alder"><data key="n_label">Black Alder</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: A. barbata C. A. Mey., A. glutinosa subsp. barbata (C. A. Mey.) Yalt., A. glutinosa var. barbata (C. A. Mey.) Ledeb., Betula alnus var. glutinosa L., B. glutinosa (L.) Lam.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BLACK ALDER</data><data key="n_page">82</data><data key="n_scientific">Alnus glutinosa (L.) Gaertn.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM). None reported in PDR.“Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) (but PH2 designates no specific quantified dosage! JAD). Regrettably, PHR and still PH2 report hypericin, probably meaning hyperoside. Hyperoside is what was reported in my CRC Handbook of Medicinal Herbs (1985) and in Hager’s Handbuch der Pharmazeutischen Praxis, ed. 2. So far, I have not yet found hypericin reliably reported outside the genus Hypericum, but hyperoside is widely reported.</data></node>
