<node id="hierba:vaccinium-angustifolia-aiton-and-corymbosum-l-blueberry"><data key="n_label">Blueberry</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: V. brittonii Porter ex C. Bicknell, V. lamarckii Camp, V. pennsylvanicum Lam.; V. constablaei A. Gray.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BLUEBERRY</data><data key="n_page">105</data><data key="n_scientific">Vaccinium angustifolia Aiton and corymbosum L.</data><data key="n_safety">+++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+++</data><data key="n_dosages">3 tbsp dried fruit chewed (APA); 1–2 tsp chopped leaf/cup water, up to 6 ×/day (APA).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP). None reported for the berry.</data><data key="n_extracts">Wild blueberries may have 25–30 different anthocyanins in high concentrations. Cooking damages the anthocyanins (JNU). Anthocyanins and polyphenols in berries of several Ribes, Rubus, and Vaccinium spp. have in vitro antiradical activity on chemically generated superoxide radicals. The extracts also inhibit xanthine oxidase. All crude extracts were highly active toward chemically generated superoxide radicals. Ribes nigrum extracts exhibited most activity, being the richest in both anthocyanins and polyphenols. But Ribes rubrum extracts seem to contain more active substances (X1332092).</data></node>
<node id="act:neuronogenic"><data key="n_label">Neuronogenic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:dementia"><data key="n_label">Dementia</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 18 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:lobelia-siphilitica-l-blue-cardinal-flower"><data key="n_label">Blue Cardinal Flower</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BLUE CARDINAL FLOWER</data><data key="n_page">105</data><data key="n_scientific">Lobelia siphilitica L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data></node>
<node id="hierba:caulophyllum-thalictroides-blue-cohosh"><data key="n_label">Blue Cohosh</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BLUE COHOSH</data><data key="n_page">106</data><data key="n_scientific">Caulophyllum thalictroides (L.) Michx.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">40–200 mg/day (APA); 0.3–2 g rhizome 3 ×/day (CAN); 1/2 –1 tsp fresh root (PED); 0.25–0.5 g dry root (PED); 0.5 g dry root: 3 g alcohol/2 ml water (PED); 0.3–2 g powdered root (PNC); 0.3–2 ml liquid root extract (PNC); 0.3–2.0 ml liquid extract (1:1 in 90% alcohol) (CAN); 2–4 ml tincture (1:10 in 60% alcohol) (CAN).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2b (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). CAN caution that the poisonous seeds will irritate the GI tract. Because it is reputed to be abortifacient and to affect the menstrual cycle, its use in pregnancy and lactation is to be avoided. May interfere with angina therapy (CAN). Anagyrines may be teratogenic (PHR). Canadian regulations do not allow blue cohosh as a nonmedicinal ingredient for oral use products (Michols, 1995). The alcoholic extract was uterotonic in guinea pig; caulosaponin is oxytocic to rat uterus in vivo; even low-potency homeopathic produce follicular and endometrial changes likened to inhibition of ovulation; administration of this homeopathic preparation interrupted implantation (De Smet et al., 1993).</data></node>
