<node id="hierba:dictamnus-albus-l-burning-bush"><data key="n_label">Burning Bush, Dittany, Gasplant</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: D. caucasicus (Fisch. &amp; C. A. Mey.) Grossh., D. fraxinellus Pers., D. fraxinellus var. caucasicus Fisch. &amp; C. A. Mey.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BURNING BUSH, DITTANY, GASPLANT</data><data key="n_page">128</data><data key="n_scientific">Dictamnus albus L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">20 g herb/liter water (PH2); 1 tsp root/2 glasses water (PH2). Strangely, PH2 suggests one g fresh herb or 2 g dry herb/cup water (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). Mutagenic, phototoxic, uterotonic.</data></node>
<node id="hierba:bidens-tripartita-l-burr-marigold"><data key="n_label">Burr Marigold, Water Agrimony</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: B. comosa (A. Gray) Wiegand, B. connata Muhl. ex Willd., B. connata var. comosa A. Gray.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BURR MARIGOLD, WATER AGRIMONY</data><data key="n_page">129</data><data key="n_scientific">Bidens tripartita L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) (but PH2 designates no specific quantified dosage! JAD).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:ruscus-aculeatus-l-butchers-broom"><data key="n_label">Butcher’S Broom</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BUTCHER’S BROOM</data><data key="n_page">130</data><data key="n_scientific">Ruscus aculeatus L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">300-mg tablets (APA); 7–11 mg ruscogenin (or neoruscogenin + ruscogenin)/day or equivalent in raw extract (KOM; PH2); 1–2 tbsp fresh root (PED); 1.5–3 g dry root (PED); 2 g dry root :10 ml alcohol/10 ml water (PED); 1 g extract 3 ×/day (SKY); StX (50–100 mg ruscogenin)/day) (SKY).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP; SKY). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). Commission E reports rhizome permitted for oral use. No contraindications or interactions. Adverse effects: rarely gastric complaints, nausea, queasiness (AEH; KOM; PHR; PH2). Ruscogenins and neoruscogenins, similar to diosgenin, are responsible for decreasing inflammation and vascular permeability (SKY). Saponins are antiaggregant, antiinflammatory, capillarifortificant, and diuretic (PED).</data></node>
<node id="act:alpha-adrenergic"><data key="n_label">Alpha-Adrenergic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:hypertriglyceridemia"><data key="n_label">Hypertriglyceridemia</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:petasites-hybridus-butterbur"><data key="n_label">Butterbur</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Tussilago hybrida L.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">BUTTERBUR</data><data key="n_page">131</data><data key="n_scientific">Petasites hybridus (L.) P. Gaertn. et al. and P. officinalis Moench</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">4.5–7 g/day dry herb (KOM; PH2; SHT); 1.2–2 g powdered herb/cup water, 2–3 ×/day (HH2; PH2). “Teas should not be used” (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Contains pyrrolizidine alkaloids (PAs). Not recommended due to PAs and sesquiterpene lactones (PNC). Daily dose should not exceed 1 µg PAs; do not dose more than 4–6 weeks a year (SHT). Botanically similar to Tussilago farfara. Adequate data about PA content not available. PAs are toxic to humans, with liver damage with cirrhosis and ascites, or seneciosis, or veno-occlusive disease (VOD) reported in almost all cases of severe or fatal intoxications, from intakes of 0.5–3.3 mg/kg (AEH). Commission E reports all plant parts contain hepatotoxic, genotoxic, and carcinogenic PAs. Contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation (AEH). Gruenwald (PHR) makes the following contradictory statements, back-to-back, first his template, “No health hazards or side effects are known in conjunction with the proper administration of designated therapeutic dosages.” Then, the conflicting statement, “One should entirely forgo any administration of the drug, due to the presence of pyrrolizidine alkaloids with hepatotoxic and carcinogenic effects in the parts of the plant above ground, as even mere traces of the alkaloids present a danger” (PHR).</data><data key="n_extracts">Ethanolic extract antispasmodic IC50 = 1.7 mg/ml (very weak cf atropine 1/1,000,000th of papaverine 1/1000th). LD50 dry drug 870 mg/kg ivn guinea pig, tincture 1250 mg/kg (HH2), LD50 root extract 2500 orl guinea pig (HH2), LD50 root extract 60 mg/kg ivn guinea pig (HH2).</data></node>
<node id="act:antilithic"><data key="n_label">Antilithic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 11 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
