<node id="hierba:andira-inermis-cabbage-bark"><data key="n_label">Cabbage Bark</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: A. excelsa Kunth, A. jamaicensis Urb., A. racemosa Lam. ex J. St.-Hil., Geoffroea inermis W. Wright, Vouacapoua inermis (W. Wright) A. Lyons.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CABBAGE BARK</data><data key="n_page">134</data><data key="n_scientific">Andira inermis (W. Wright) Kunth ex DC.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; APA; KOM; PHR). Contains berberine and biochanin-A, both of which have many biologically important activities (WO2).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:calliandra-grandiflora-cabeza-de-angel"><data key="n_label">Cabeza De Angel</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Aneslia strigillosa Britton &amp; Rose, C. anomala (Kunth) J. F. Macbr., C. strigillosa (Britton &amp; Rose) Standl. ex Leavenw., Inga anomala Kunth, Mimosa grandiflora L’Hér.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CABEZA DE ANGEL</data><data key="n_page">134</data><data key="n_scientific">Calliandra grandiflora (L’Her) Benth.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_downsides">“Classed as a narcotic hypnotic” (CRC).</data></node>
<node id="ind:eye-disease"><data key="n_label">Eye Disease</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:theobroma-cacao-l-cacao"><data key="n_label">Cacao</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CACAO</data><data key="n_page">134</data><data key="n_scientific">Theobroma cacao L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">1–2 tsp cocoa/cup water and/or milk (APA).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). Not approved (KOM). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages or with consuming normal amounts of chocolate products” (PH2) (but PH2 designates no dosage level and does not define normal usage! JAD). Commission E reports contraindications for seed and seed shell: hypersensitivity; adverse effects: allergic reactions with skin manifestations and migraine (AEH). Patients with irritable bowel syndrome should avoid cocoa-containing products (APA). Cocoa butter clogs pores, causing blackheads in animals (APA). Biogenic amines (phenylethylamine, serotonin, tryptamine, and tyramine) can trigger migraine attacks (free amines total 750–1440 ppm in fermented beans). Even caffeine can trigger headaches in naive subjects. Theophylline is present in traces only in unroasted beans, which disappear with roasting (HHB). Enzymes such as hydrolase, lipase, and proteinase are deactivated in roasting. If caffeine from coffee works for hyperkinesis (ADD), a big if, why not caffeine from chocolate, or tea, both of which may have even more health benefits via their catechin-tannins? Milk chocolate may have 2000 ppm caffeine, bitter chocolate 4000 ppm (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="act:broncholytic"><data key="n_label">Broncholytic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
