<node id="ind:screw-worm"><data key="n_label">Screw Worm</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:melaleuca-leucadendra-l-cajeput"><data key="n_label">Cajeput</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Kajuputi leucadendra (L.) Rusby, Myrtus leucadendra L. After visiting Australia and conferring with teatree experts, I am convinced that most of the published studies are taxonomically confused. I am using the Cajeput in the sense of the USDA, not in the narrower Australian sense of cajuput, which follows. The lazy way out, lumping the species, won’t solve the taxonomic quandary.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CAJEPUT</data><data key="n_page">135</data><data key="n_scientific">Melaleuca leucadendra L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">Oil used as a monopreparation, externally as a 5% alcohol solution (KOM); topical use only, dosage not specified (PH2); 0.05–0.2 ml Cajuput oil (PNC); 0.3–2 ml Cajuput spirit (PNC).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). No health hazards or side effects noted with proper administration (PHR; PH2). Do not apply near nostrils of pediatrics, may cause serious glottal spasms (KOM; PHR). More than 10 g cajuput oil may induce life-threatening intoxication, due to cineole, with circulatory and respiratory disorders, hypotension, and possible collapse (PH2). Topical and vaginal irritation have been reported from similar oils from other species of Melaleuca, but the topical use of the oil has not generally been associated with toxicity. Still, the topical use cannot be recommended at this time (LRNP, January 1991). As with most EOs, this one may induce dermatosis in sensitive individuals.</data></node>
<node id="act:suppurative"><data key="n_label">Suppurative</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 10 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:skin"><data key="n_label">Skin</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:slipped-disk"><data key="n_label">Slipped Disk</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:melaleuca-cajuputi-powell-cajuput"><data key="n_label">Cajuput</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: M. minor Sm. American authors (JFM) treat cajeput as Melaleuca leucadendron L. or M. quinquenervia. Few, if any, of the chemical studies are vouchered.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CAJUPUT</data><data key="n_page">136</data><data key="n_scientific">Melaleuca cajuputi Powell</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data></node>
<node id="hierba:crescentia-cujete-l-calabash-tree"><data key="n_label">Calabash Tree</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CALABASH TREE</data><data key="n_page">136</data><data key="n_scientific">Crescentia cujete L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_downsides">Not recommended for internal consumption (TRA). Fruit pulp may be carcinogenic; ingestion may produce severe diarrhea (TRA).</data></node>
<node id="ind:spider-bite"><data key="n_label">Spider Bite</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:calamintha-nepeta-calamint"><data key="n_label">Calamint</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Clinopodium nepeta (L.) Kuntze, Melissa nepeta L., Satureja calamintha (L.) Scheele, S. nepeta (L.) Scheele. PH2 data seem to have been in part derived from HHB, which is covering C. officinalis or C. montana, neither of which is in the USDA database. I use only C. nepeta data below.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CALAMINT</data><data key="n_page">137</data><data key="n_scientific">Calamintha nepeta (L.) Savi.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP).“Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:acorus-calamus-l-calamus"><data key="n_label">Calamus</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CALAMUS</data><data key="n_page">137</data><data key="n_scientific">Acorus calamus L.</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">XXX</data><data key="n_dosages">2–7 g powdered root (10–15 as emetic) (KAP).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1, only for the asaronefree diploid rhizome. Class 2b for the triploid and tetraploid (AHP). CAN reports that the betaasarone in the oil is carcinogenic and nephrotoxic, and may cause convulsions. AHP hints that the risks, like those of safrole, may not be as serious as the press would have us believe. Oil carcinogenic, convulsant; kidney damage and tremors. May potentiate MAOI therapy (CAN). And a real bombshell for uncut aromatherapists, “In general, the topical application of any undiluted EO is not recommend” (CAN). EO LD50 = 777 orl rat; LD50 = 221 ipr rat (CAN); LD50 = &gt;5000 der guinea pig (CAN).</data></node>
<node id="act:cns-sedative"><data key="n_label">CNS-Sedative</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
