<node id="hierba:umbellularia-californica-california-bay"><data key="n_label">California Bay</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Tetranthera californica Hook. &amp; Arn.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CALIFORNIA BAY</data><data key="n_page">140</data><data key="n_scientific">Umbellularia californica (Hook. and Arn.) Nutt.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data></node>
<node id="hierba:schinus-molle-l-california-peppertree"><data key="n_label">California Peppertree</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Schinus huygan Molina.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CALIFORNIA PEPPERTREE</data><data key="n_page">141</data><data key="n_scientific">Schinus molle L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">For gargles and external washes 30 g drug/500 ml water.</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1; GI irritant; though toxic in quantity, still used as spice (AHP). “Health hazards not known with proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) (but PH2 designates no specific quantified dosage! JAD). Children intoxicated by fruits, with diarrhea, gastroenterosis, headache, lassitude, and nausea (CRC). LD50 (EO) = &gt;5000 mg/kg orl rat (HH2).</data></node>
<node id="ind:blennorrhagia"><data key="n_label">Blennorrhagia</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 11 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:eschscholzia-californica-cham-california-poppy"><data key="n_label">California Poppy</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CALIFORNIA POPPY</data><data key="n_page">141</data><data key="n_scientific">Eschscholzia californica Cham.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">1–2 ml liquid extract (PHR); 2–3 g in tea, to 4 cups/day (AHP); 1 tsp dry root and/or herb/cup water 2–3 ×/day; 30–40 drops tincture 2–3 ×/day (WAF).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2b (AHP). Commission E reports aerial parts not permitted for therapeutic use. Use during pregnancy should be avoided as the major alkaloid cryptopine shows a stimulating effect on guinea pig uterus in vitro (AEH). Do not use during pregnancy; do not use with MAOI antidepressants (WAM).</data><data key="n_extracts">Californidine reportedly antispasmodic, anxiolytic, sedative, and soporific. Cryptopine uterotonic at 1 ppm (HHB).</data></node>
<node id="ind:ocd"><data key="n_label">OCD</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 4 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:cinnamomum-camphora-camphor"><data key="n_label">Camphor</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Laurus camphora L. Many books confuse camphor the tree with camphor the chemical.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CAMPHOR</data><data key="n_page">142</data><data key="n_scientific">Cinnamomum camphora (L.) J. Presl</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_dosages">Topical ointments with 3–11% camphor, no more than 4 ×/day (APA); 25% ointments, &lt;6% pediatric (PIP); 30–300 mg/day camphor (KOM; PIP); 1–10% camphor spirit extract (PIP); maximum daily dose, 30 mg camphor (PNC).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2b, 2d (AHP). Topical use only; never ingest camphor (APA). Do not use on children’s faces. Do not apply near children’s nostrils (AEH; KOM; PIP). Not for long-term use. Do not exceed recommended dose. May cause confusion, delirium, eczema, epileptiform convulsions (constant in animals), excitement, headache, nausea, and rarely death, by asphyxia and collapse (WO2). Contraindicated in broken or burned skin (KOM; PIP). Commission E reports contraindications (for external use—damaged skin; and adverse effects: contact eczema (AEH).</data></node>
