<node id="hierba:hydnocarpus-spp-chaulmoogra"><data key="n_label">Chaulmoogra</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CHAULMOOGRA</data><data key="n_page">179</data><data key="n_scientific">Hydnocarpus spp.</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_dosages">0.3 ml oil 3 ×/day, to a max dose of 4 ml/day (HHB); 1–4 ml oil (KAP); 1–3 g powdered seed (KAP).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). Overdoses may induce CNS ups and downs, cramping, dyspnea, headache, myalgia, nausea, nephrosis, respiratory problems, and visual disorders (MAD).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:sechium-edule-chayote"><data key="n_label">Chayote</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Chayota edulis Jacq., Sicyos edulis Jacq.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CHAYOTE</data><data key="n_page">180</data><data key="n_scientific">Sechium edule (Jacq.) Sw.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">Boil 3 leaves with 5 of Casimiroa for high blood pressure (JFM).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM; PH2). Peeling the fruit may cause inflammation or even numbness if a number are peeled. Leaf extracts exert transient depressor effect when injected in laboratory animals.</data></node>
<node id="hierba:terminalia-chebula-retz-chebulic-myrobalan"><data key="n_label">Chebulic Myrobalan, Haritali</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CHEBULIC MYROBALAN, HARITALI</data><data key="n_page">180</data><data key="n_scientific">Terminalia chebula Retz.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">3–9 g/day (HH2; PH2); 1.5–6 g powdered bark (KAP); 56–112 ml decoction (KAP).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2d. Contraindicated in acute coughs, acute diarrhea, and early stage dysentery (AHP). As one of the best sources of tannin (over 30%), chebula may have many of the activities proven for tannin (see FNF).</data></node>
<node id="act:atpase-inhibitor"><data key="n_label">ATPase-Inhibitor</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
