<node id="ind:spina-bifida"><data key="n_label">Spina Bifida</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:stellaria-media-chickweed"><data key="n_label">Chickweed</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Alsine media L.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CHICKWEED</data><data key="n_page">183</data><data key="n_scientific">Stellaria media (L.) Vill.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">3 (389 mg) capsules 3 ×/day (APA); 1 tsp plant juice 6–8 ×/day (MAD); 2–3 tsp herb in cold or hot tea (MAD); 0.25–0.5 cup fresh herb (PED); 6–12 g dry herb (PED); 9 g dry herb:45 ml alcohol/45 ml water (PED); 1–4 ml liquid herb extract (PNC); 1–5 ml tincture/day (SKY).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP).“Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) (but PH2 designates no specific quantified dosage! JAD). None reported (SKY). One alleged case of nitrate toxicity (AHP). Which raises a question: If high nitrate/high nitrites are dangerous in combination with Viagra, maybe that plant group once called Centrospermae (which includes, e.g., chickweed, lambs quarter, poke, pigweed, purslane), might also chickweed be contraindicated with Viagra? Alternatively, they might, along with garlic, make a viagroid nitric-oxide-generating mess of greens. Saponins enhance transdermal absorption of other chemicals and may solubilize other medicines internally. I am still trying to confirm European studies that cited genistein in chickweed.</data></node>
<node id="hierba:cichorium-intybus-l-chicory"><data key="n_label">Chicory</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: C. intybus var. foliosum Hegi, C. intybus var. sativum (Bisch.) Janch.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CHICORY</data><data key="n_page">184</data><data key="n_scientific">Cichorium intybus L.</data><data key="n_safety">+++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+++</data><data key="n_dosages">3 g root (KOM); 3–5 g powdered root (PHR); 2–4 g/150–250 ml water (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP).“Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). Commission E reports contraindications of hypersensitivity to chicory and other Asteraceae and adverse effects of rare allergic skin reactions. Patients with bilestones or gallstones should first consult a physician (AEH; KOM). Cadot et al. report “a first case of occupational allergy to chicory (Cichorium intybus) in a vegetable wholesaler. Symptoms occurred after oral, cutaneous, or inhalatory exposure.” Reactions were also noted after ingestion of the botanically related endive (Cichorium endivia) and lettuce (Latuca satiua). The responsible protein allergen was identified by SDS-PAGE and immunoblot with no cross-reactivity with mugwort (Artemisia vulgaris), ryegrass (Lolium perenne), and birch (Betula verrucosa) pollen, suggesting the vegetable is the allergenic material (Cadot et al., 1996). Carcinogens, viz. 1,2-benzoperylene, 3,4,benzopyrone, and floranthene occur in chicory, increasing on roasting, especially above 175° (WOI). Ingestion of 100 g may cause slight bradycardia.</data></node>
<node id="act:antispermatogenic"><data key="n_label">Antispermatogenic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 6 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:hypercholesterolemia"><data key="n_label">Hypercholesterolemia</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 4 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:melia-azedarach-l-chinaberry"><data key="n_label">Chinaberry</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: M. azedarach var. japonica (G. Don) Makino, M. toosendan Siebold &amp; Zucc.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CHINABERRY</data><data key="n_page">185</data><data key="n_scientific">Melia azedarach L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">5 g leaf/500 g water for parasites (JFM); 1–1.9 g root for parasites (JFM); 1–2 g powdered root (KAP); 1–2 g powdered fruit (KAP); 56–112 ml bark decoction (KAP); 56–112 ml leaf decoction (KAP); 56–112 ml root bark decoction (KAP); 4–12 ml tincture (KAP); 28–84 ml flower tea (KAP); 4–10 minims oil (KAP).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 3 (AHP). Not covered (PHR). Fatality reported in a child who ate the berries (DEP). Six to eight seeds said to cause choleraic symptoms, nausea, and spasm, sometimes followed by death (DEP). LD50 (50% ethanolic extract) = 250 mg/kg ipr mouse (MPI).</data></node>
<node id="act:pulicide"><data key="n_label">Pulicide</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 5 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:fontanelle"><data key="n_label">Fontanelle</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:hibiscus-rosa-sinensis-l-china-rose"><data key="n_label">China Rose</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CHINA ROSE</data><data key="n_page">186</data><data key="n_scientific">Hibiscus rosa-sinensis L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_downsides">Internal uses contraindicated in small children and pregnant or lactating women (TRA). Hydroethanolic extract LD50 1000 ppm (TRA).</data></node>
<node id="act:embryotoxic"><data key="n_label">Embryotoxic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 6 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:huperzia-serrata-chinese-club-moss"><data key="n_label">Chinese Club Moss</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CHINESE CLUB MOSS</data><data key="n_page">186</data><data key="n_scientific">Huperzia serrata</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">200–300 µg huperzine A/day.</data><data key="n_downsides">Pregnant women, and people with hypertension or pulmonary problems due to increased bronchial secretions should not take it. Said to have a high therapeutic index with few side effects (ABS).</data><data key="n_extracts">Huperzine A reversibly binds acetylcholinesterase (AChE), “preventing it from breaking down acetylcholine, and thus preventing a deficiency and improving mental function” (ABS). It binds with a half-life of 4.8 hours. Its effect may persist 8 hours, longer than donepezil and tacrine, FDA-approved drugs for Alzheimer’s. Huperzine A’s distinct mode of action is said to maintain and improve memory.</data></node>
<node id="act:antiglutamate"><data key="n_label">Antiglutamate</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:myasthenia-gravis"><data key="n_label">Myasthenia Gravis</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 4 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:trichosanthes-kirilowii-maxim-chinese-cucumber"><data key="n_label">Chinese Cucumber</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CHINESE CUCUMBER</data><data key="n_page">186</data><data key="n_scientific">Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim.</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_dosages">9–12 g (FAY).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1. Rare cases of diarrhea and gastrosis (AHP). Trichosanthin is quite dangerous, leading to acute pulmonary edema, cerebral edema and hemorrhage, and myocardosis. Severe side effects, also antigenic (FAY).</data></node>
<node id="ind:choriocarcinoma"><data key="n_label">Choriocarcinoma</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
