<node id="ind:hydrocephaly"><data key="n_label">Hydrocephaly</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:psychosis"><data key="n_label">Psychosis</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 8 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:chrysanthemum-x-morifolium-ramat-chrysanthemum"><data key="n_label">Chrysanthemum, Mum</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Anthemis grandiflorum Ramat., A. stipulacea Moench, Chrysanthemum sinense Sabine, C. stipulaceum (Moench) W. Wight, Dendranthema grandiflorum (Ramat.) Kitam., D. morifolium Tzvelev., Matricaria morifolia Ramat.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CHRYSANTHEMUM, MUM</data><data key="n_page">193</data><data key="n_scientific">Chrysanthemum x morifolium Ramat.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">4.5–12 g flower (FAY).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP). Not covered (KOM; PH2). Unfortunately, Tillotson has mixed feverfew in with the mums, such that I don’t know to which he means his warning to apply, “Should not be used during pregnancy as it is a uterine stimulant. ...Some scientists voice concern about potential allergic reactions or cross reactivity with blood thinning agents such as warfarin or Ticlopidine” (AKT).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:eryngium-foetidum-l-cilantro"><data key="n_label">Cilantro</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CILANTRO</data><data key="n_page">194</data><data key="n_scientific">Eryngium foetidum L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">Take juice of leaves before meals for anorexia (JFM); infuse root in rum or wine as a vermifuge (JFM).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). The emmenagogue folklore might suggest caution for use, if at all, by pregnant women.</data></node>
<node id="hierba:cinchona-pubescens-vahl-cinchona"><data key="n_label">Cinchona, Quinine, Redbark</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: C. succirubra Pav. ex Klotzsch. USDA prefers this species be called redbark; and that C. callisaya be called quinine, or yellowbark, or yellow cinchona, or yellow quinine. Both seem to share many morphological, chemical, and folkloric attributes. They are probably pretty tough to distinguish when reduced to powdered bark. Consider the entries below as likely to accrue to one as the other. FEL entries below are sometimes based on medical uses of quinine at the beginning of the 20th century.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CINCHONA, QUININE, REDBARK</data><data key="n_page">195</data><data key="n_scientific">Cinchona pubescens Vahl</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">1 tsp bark boiled 5–10 min, 3 ×/day (MPG); 1–3 g bark (KOM); 10 grains for hay fever, hemicrania, neuralgia, pertussis, splenomegaly (MPI); 0.3–1 g powdered bark (PNC); 0.3–1 ml liquid extract (PNC); 0.3–1 ml cinchona extract (PNC); 2–4 ml cinchona tincture (PNC); maximum dose 50 mg alkaloids (PNC).</data><data key="n_downsides">Commission E reports contraindications; pregnancy and hypersensitivity; adverse effects: allergic reactions, rarely thrombocytopenia; and interaction: potentiation of coumarin derivatives. Other sources report contraindication for GI-ulcer, adverse effects for allergic skin reactions and fever. Overdosing or prolonged use may produce toxic effects (AEH). 8–20 g quinine may be fatal in humans (WO2).</data><data key="n_extracts">Cinchonidine, cinchonine, hydroquinine, quinidine, and quinine all oxytocic in experimental animals at levels of 0.5–10 mg/kg (WO2). Cinchonidine, cinchonine, quinidine, and quinine are some of the antipyretic compounds in this growing medicine chest called quinine. From an amebicidal point of view, compounds modified from quinine and quinidine were not as potent as emetine and derivatives (I might mix my ipecac and quinine in Amazonia). Chloro9-desoxy-quinine and quinidine were least active, while 9-desoxy-dihydro derivatives showed fair activity. More compounds that showed slight in vitro activity were also active in vivo, exceptions being 9-desoxy-dihydro quinine and quinidine, which were active in vivo (MPI).</data></node>
<node id="act:schizonticide"><data key="n_label">Schizonticide</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
