<node id="ind:lung"><data key="n_label">Lung</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:dioscorea-batatas-decne-cinnamon-vine"><data key="n_label">Cinnamon-Vine, Chinese Yam</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Dioscorea opposita auct.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CINNAMON-VINE, CHINESE YAM</data><data key="n_page">198</data><data key="n_scientific">Dioscorea batatas Decne.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">None found.</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM; PH2). None found.</data></node>
<node id="act:nootropic"><data key="n_label">Nootropic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:salvia-sclarea-l-clary"><data key="n_label">Clary</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: S. sclarea var. turkestaniana Mottet</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CLARY</data><data key="n_page">199</data><data key="n_scientific">Salvia sclarea L.</data><data key="n_safety">+++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+++</data><data key="n_dosages">Food farmacy? To be eaten for cancer (JLH).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP). Not covered (KOM; PHR; PH2). LD50 EO = &gt;5000–5600 mg/kg orl rat (HH2); LD50 EO = &gt;520–550 mg/kg ipr mouse (HH2); LD50 EO = 740 mg/kg ipr rat (HH2); 2000–&gt;5000 mg/kg dml dog (HH2).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:galium-aparine-l-cleavers"><data key="n_label">Cleavers</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CLEAVERS</data><data key="n_page">199</data><data key="n_scientific">Galium aparine L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">1 oz leaf/pint water 3 ×/day (APA); 5–10 ml herb tincture 3 ×/day (APA); 2–4 g herb as tea 3 ×/day (CAN); 3–4 tsp (3.3–4.4 g)/day in tea (MAD); 2–4 ml liquid extract (1:1 in 25% ethanol) 3 ×/day (CAN; PNC); 2–4 ml liquid herb extract (PNC); 3–15 ml expressed juice 3 ×/daily (CAN); 3.75–7.5 g expressed sap (MAD); 4 tsp (3.3–4.4 g) in 2 glasses water (HH2; PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not recorded for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). None known (WAM). Diabetics should use the expressed juice only with caution. Excessive use of cleavers should be avoided, especially during pregnancy (CAN). Lower arterial blood pressure in canines with no slowing of heart and no toxic effects. Like other iridoids, asperuloside is a mild laxative (PNC). It can be converted to prostanoid intermediates. I was surprised to find harmine, 1-hydroxydesoxypeganin, 8-hydroxy-2,3-dehydrodesoxypeganin, and protopine listed for this plant by the Herbal PDR (Gruenwald, et al., 2000). I am reluctant to enter them in my database without checking their original sources.</data></node>
