<node id="hierba:senna-occidentalis-coffee-senna"><data key="n_label">Coffee Senna</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Cassia occidentalis L.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">COFFEE SENNA</data><data key="n_page">208</data><data key="n_scientific">Senna occidentalis (L.) Link.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_downsides">May cause cardiomyopathy in rabbits. Chrysarobin and toxalbumin may cause kidney and liver damage. Contains the COX-2Inhibitor, apigenin.</data></node>
<node id="act:antidiuretic"><data key="n_label">Antidiuretic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 10 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:bed-wetting"><data key="n_label">Bed Wetting</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:bone-ache"><data key="n_label">Bone Ache</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:cola-spp-cola"><data key="n_label">Cola</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">COLA</data><data key="n_page">209</data><data key="n_scientific">Cola spp.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">1–2 tsp powdered seed/cup water, up to 3 ×/day (APA); 1–3 g powdered seed (PNC); 2–6 g seed/day (PHR); 0.5–1 dropper full seed tincture or concentrate (APA); 1–3 g herb as tea 3 ×/day (CAN); 0.6–1.2 ml liquid extract (1:1 in 60% ethanol) (CAN); 1–4 ml tincture (1:5 in 60% alcohol) (CAN); 25–750 mg cola extract (PHR); 1–4 ml kola tincture (PNC); 10–30 g cola tincture (PHR); 60–120 g cola wine; 0.6–1.2 ml liquid seed extract (PNC).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2b, 2d (AHP). Commission E reports contraindications: gastric and duodenal ulcers; adverse effects: trouble sleeping, hyperexcitability, nervousness; interactions: effect enhanced by psychoanaleptic drugs and caffeinecontaining beverages (AEH). CNS stimulant, GI irritant. Contraindicated in high blood pressure, and gastric and duodenal ulcers. Not recommended for excessive or prolonged use (they seem to say this about most caffeine-containing plants) (AHP). CAN cautions that xanthine-containing beverages may cause anxiety, insomnia, palpitations, tremors, and withdrawal headaches. Because of the caffeine, consumption should be restricted in pregnancy and lactation, and in patients with hypertension and cardiac problems. “As with all xanthine containing beverages, excessive consumption by lactating mothers should be avoided” (CAN). Cola-containing beverages are said to “provide active doses of caffeine” (CAN). Caffeine has many reported activities, many perhaps shared synergistically with theophylline and theobromine.</data></node>
<node id="act:thymoleptic"><data key="n_label">Thymoleptic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 8 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
