<node id="ind:dysphagia"><data key="n_label">Dysphagia</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:tonsillosis"><data key="n_label">Tonsillosis</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:aquilegia-vulgaris-l-columbine"><data key="n_label">Columbine</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">COLUMBINE</data><data key="n_page">214</data><data key="n_scientific">Aquilegia vulgaris L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">Only homeopathic doses given (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). None known (PHR). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). Poisoning due to HCN not observed (PHR). Human fatalities reported (LEL).</data></node>
<node id="ind:globus-hystericus"><data key="n_label">Globus Hystericus</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:photosensitivity"><data key="n_label">Photosensitivity</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:symphytum-spp-comfrey"><data key="n_label">Comfrey</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">COMFREY</data><data key="n_page">214</data><data key="n_scientific">Symphytum spp.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">Do not use (APA); do not use root (JAD); 2–4 g root as tea 3 ×/day (CAN); 2 tsp (= ~7.4 g) root in hot tea (MAD); 2–4 ml liquid root extract (PNC); 2–4 ml liquid extract (1:1 in 25% ethanol) 3 ×/day (CAN); 2–8 ml liquid leaf extract (1:1 in 25% alcohol) 3 ×/day (CAN); 2–8 g leaf in tea 3 ×/day (CAN); 0.25–0.5 cup fresh leaf (PED); 6–12 g dry leaf (PED); 9 g dry leaf:45 ml alcohol/45 ml water (PED); 1–3 cups tea/day (5–10 g herb) remembering PAs (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2a, 2b, 2c, 2d. Longterm use discouraged (AHP). Commission E reports the herb, leaf, and root permitted for external use only. Skin should be intact and pregnant users should first consult physician. External dosage of pyrrolizidine alkaloids (PAs) maximum 100 g/day for a maximum 4–6 weeks/year (AEH). Comfrey root may cause liver damage if taken internally (WAM). Contains PAs. Internal use may cause severe hepatic damage. PAs are toxic to humans, with liver damage with cirrhosis and ascites, or seneciosis, or veno-occlusive disease (VOD) reported in almost all cases of severe or fatal intoxications, from intakes of 0.5 mg/kg to 3.3 mg/kg (AEH1). Chronic comfrey use implicated in at least one instance of hepatic VOD (PNC). Effective July 1996, the AHP Board of Trustees recommends that all products with botanical ingredient(s) that contain toxic PAs, including Borago officinalis, display the following cautionary statement on the label, “For external use only. Do not apply to broken or abraded skin. Do not use when nursing” (AHP). CAN cautions the PAs are genotoxic, carcinogenic, and hepatotoxic. Because of the PAs, its use in pregnancy and lactation is to be avoided. Animal studies document placental transfer and secretion into breast milk of unsaturated PAs (CAN). May speed up metabolism of other drugs (stimulates metabolism of aminopyrine-N-demethylase, a drug metabolizing enzyme) (CAN). Internal use for more than 4–6 weeks is discouraged (SHT). Canadians do not allow in food (Blackburn, 1993). “No human being or animal should eat, drink, or take comfrey in any form” (Br. Med. J. 6163: 596; 1979). According to studies reported in the Lawrence Review of Natural Products, rats fed comfrey roots or leaves for 600 days developed hepatocellular adenomas, with signs of liver toxicity developing within 180 days. Urinary bladder tumors developed also, even in those on the lowest levels of comfrey. The incidence of liver tumors was higher with dietary roots than with dietary comfrey leaves. Alkaloids of Russian comfrey caused chronic liver damage and pancreatic islet cell tumors after 2 years administration in animal models (LRNP, October 1990).</data><data key="n_extracts">Extracts antiinflammatory in vitro and in vivo, perhaps due to rosmarinic acid (PNC). Allantoin a well known dermatological agent (PNC). Aqueous extract stimulates release of prostaglandin-like material from rat gastric mucosa (PNC). Two nonhepatotoxic PAs, platyphylline and sarracine, have been used for GI hypermotility and peptic ulceration. Yes, aqueous extracts increase survival time of mice with spontaneous tumors, and decrease tumor growth, and have antimutagenic activity (PNC). Is comfrey more likely to cause, cure, or prevent cancer? This is what we really should be studying.</data></node>
<node id="act:antileukocyte"><data key="n_label">Antileukocyte</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="act:callus-promoter"><data key="n_label">Callus-Promoter</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:cancer-bone"><data key="n_label">Cancer, bone</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 6 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:duodenal-ulcer"><data key="n_label">Duodenal Ulcer</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:epicondylosis"><data key="n_label">Epicondylosis</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:tendovaginosis"><data key="n_label">Tendovaginosis</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:rhamnus-cathartica-l-common-buckthorn"><data key="n_label">Common Buckthorn</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">COMMON BUCKTHORN</data><data key="n_page">216</data><data key="n_scientific">Rhamnus cathartica L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">0.6–5 g fruit. (HHB); 0–20 berries (MAD); 15–30 g juice; children 0.5–1 tsp syrup (MAD); 4 g dry fruit/cup tea, 2–5 g drug/day corresponding to 20–30 mg hydroxyanthracene (calculated as glucofrangulin A) (PH2); 2–4 ml buckthorn berry syrup (PNC).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2b (AHP). Commission E reports for fruit, contraindications, adverse effects, and interactions of anthranoid laxatives (AEH). Contraindicated in obstruction of the bowel or intestines, acute inflammation of the bowels as in appendicitis, colitis, and Crohn’s disease. Do not use if under 12 years of age, or pregnant (KOM; PH2). See anthranoids in introductory section. Berry extract induce tumor necrosis in mice (PNC).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:narcissus-pseudonarcissus-l-common-daffodil"><data key="n_label">Common Daffodil</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">COMMON DAFFODIL</data><data key="n_page">217</data><data key="n_scientific">Narcissus pseudonarcissus L.</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_dosages">1/4 to 10 drops tincture (bulb macerated in 98% ethanol) (FEL); 10–60 grains powdered flower or bulb (FEL).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) (but PH2 designates no specific quantified dosage! JAD). Potentially allergenic. Intoxication (confused with onion bulbs) can cause CNS disorders, diarrhea, GI irritation, salivation, and vomiting (FEL; PH2).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:lithospermum-officinale-l-common-gromwell"><data key="n_label">Common Gromwell</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">COMMON GROMWELL</data><data key="n_page">217</data><data key="n_scientific">Lithospermum officinale L.</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_dosages">1 oz dry root/pint water; 1 tsp every 3 hours (FEL); 1/2 tsp powdered seed every 44–45 hours (FEL).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; PH2). If it contains the same shikonins and PAs, or nearly so, as the Chinese Lithospermum, it should probably be avoided.</data></node>
<node id="hierba:phragmites-australis-common-reed"><data key="n_label">Common Reed</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Arundo phragmites L., A. vulgaris Lam., P. communis Trin., P. communis var. longivalvis (Steud.) Miq., P. longivalvis Steud., P. vulgaris (Lam.) Crép., P. vulgaris var. longivalvis (Steud.) W. Wight.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">COMMON REED</data><data key="n_page">218</data><data key="n_scientific">Phragmites australis (Cav.) Trin. ex Steud.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">Not covered. Young sprouts eaten as a delicacy in Japan (HHB).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) (but PH2 designates no specific quantified dosage! JAD).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:marsdenia-cundurango-rchb-f-condor-plant"><data key="n_label">Condor Plant, Eagle Vine</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Marsdenia reichenbachii Triana. Often spelled Condurango (Illustrated in HH2).</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CONDOR PLANT, EAGLE VINE</data><data key="n_page">218</data><data key="n_scientific">Marsdenia cundurango Rchb. f.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">1–4 g powdered bark (PNC); 1.5 g bark/cup tea (HHB); average daily dose bark 2–4 g (HH2; PHR); 2–4 g/day (HH2); 0.2–0.5 g aqueous extract (?); 0.5–4 ml tincture (HHB); 2–5 g tincture; 2–4 g liquid extract (PHR); 2–4 ml liquid extract (PNC); 1 cup wine 30 minutes before meals (50–100 g/liter wine) (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not recorded for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). No side effects reported during therapeutic use (AEH; PHR).</data></node>
<node id="ind:atonia"><data key="n_label">Atonia</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 5 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:cancer-epithelium"><data key="n_label">Cancer, epithelium</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:holarrhena-pubescens-wall-ex-g-don-conessi"><data key="n_label">Conessi, Kurchi</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Chonemorpha antidysenterica (Roxb. ex Fleming) G. Don, Echites antidysenterica Roth, Holarrhena antidysenterica (Roxb. ex Fleming) Wall. ex A. DC., H. febrifuga Klotzsch.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CONESSI, KURCHI</data><data key="n_page">219</data><data key="n_scientific">Holarrhena pubescens Wall. ex G. Don</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">2–4 g powdered seed (KAP); 28–74 ml decoction or tea (KAP); 2–4 ml tincture (KAP); daily dose of 60–120 grains powdered bark in 3–4 portions (KAB); bark taken in decoction (WOI); doses &gt;500 mg/person may be toxic.</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (KOM; PHR; AHP; APA). Doses of conessine &gt;500 mg day may induce GI distress, insomnia, restlessness, tremors, vertigo, possibly even death from central respiratory paralysis (WBB; WOI). LD50 (50% ethanolic fruit extract) = 250 mg/kg ipr mouse (MPI). LD50 (50% ethanolic sb extract) = 1000 mg/kg ipr mouse (MPI). With at least 18 alkaloids, is the conessine most important at 4000 ppm. Conessine increases coronary outflow in isolated animal tissues. Induces narcosis in frogs and is locally anesthetic in guinea pigs, being “twice as active as cocaine.” Subcutaneous injections may induce necrosis. Conessine has antiamebic activities comparable to emetine.</data></node>
<node id="ind:trypanosoma"><data key="n_label">Trypanosoma</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:maytenus-ilicifolia-marf-ex-reissek-congorosa"><data key="n_label">Congorosa</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CONGOROSA</data><data key="n_page">220</data><data key="n_scientific">Maytenus ilicifolia Marf. ex Reissek</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">100–400 ml decoction/tea (2–5%) (HH2; PH2); 5–20 g powdered leaf (PH2); 25–100 ml tincture (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">“Hazards and/or side effects not recorded for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) Maytansines embryotoxic and teratogenic. Not for use during pregnancy. Maytenin antiseptic, antimelanomic, antitumor, and antiulcer, especially against basal cell carcinoma (PH2). Prevents ulcers in animals and humans (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="ind:hodgkins-disease"><data key="n_label">Hodgkin’s Disease</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 6 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:dorstenia-contrayerba-l-contrahierba"><data key="n_label">Contrahierba</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CONTRAHIERBA</data><data key="n_page">220</data><data key="n_scientific">Dorstenia contrayerba L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">Decoct 8 g in 180 cc sweetened water (take 2 tbsp every other hour) (JFM).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not recorded for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) Furanocoumarins may trigger phototoxicity.</data></node>
<node id="hierba:copaifera-langsdorfii-desf-copaiba"><data key="n_label">Copaiba</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">COPAIBA</data><data key="n_page">220</data><data key="n_scientific">Copaifera langsdorfii Desf.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">0.5–1 g capsule (MAD); 25–30 drops tincture 3 ×/day (MAD).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). Five grams can cause stomach pain (PH2). Large doses are laxative and emetic; causing strangury, bloody urine, and fever. The resin irritates the entire mucous membrane, imparting a peculiar odor to the urine and breath; causes an eruption resembling measles, attended with irritation and tingling (JAD).</data></node>
<node id="ind:kidney-sone"><data key="n_label">Kidney Sone</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:erythrina-fusca-lour-coral-bean"><data key="n_label">Coral Bean</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: E. glauca Willd.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CORAL BEAN</data><data key="n_page">221</data><data key="n_scientific">Erythrina fusca lour.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_downsides">“Seeds contain the alkaloid erythralin. Erysodine, erysonine, erysopine, erysothiopine, erysothiovine, erysovine, and erythraline are also reported. Those species containing indoles and isoquinolines are classed as narcotics, capable of inducing hallucinogenic stupor” (CRC).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:corallorhiza-odontorrhiza-coral-root"><data key="n_label">Coral Root</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CORAL ROOT</data><data key="n_page">221</data><data key="n_scientific">Corallorhiza odontorrhiza</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:coriandrum-sativum-l-coriander"><data key="n_label">Coriander</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CORIANDER</data><data key="n_page">222</data><data key="n_scientific">Coriandrum sativum L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">1–2 tsp crushed fruit/cup water up to 3 ×/day (APA); 3 g fruit (KOM; PHR); 0.3–1 g powdered fruit (PNC); 0.5–2 ml liquid fruit extract (PNC); 0.05–2 (they said 2, I’d have said 0.2; cf celery seed, close kin) ml EO (PNC).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP). None known (KOM). “Hazards and/or side effects not recorded for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="ind:erotomania"><data key="n_label">Erotomania</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:ptomaine"><data key="n_label">Ptomaine</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:duboisia-myoporoides-r-br-corkwood-tree"><data key="n_label">Corkwood Tree, Pituri</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CORKWOOD TREE, PITURI</data><data key="n_page">223</data><data key="n_scientific">Duboisia myoporoides R.Br.</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_downsides">“Narcotic stimulant, secondarily a hallucinogen. Although the poisoned dreams of pituri represent a flirtation with death, they are preferred to the sting of harsh reality” (CRC). See belladonna alkaloid warnings and activities.</data></node>
<node id="hierba:agrostemma-githago-l-corn-cockle"><data key="n_label">Corn Cockle</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CORN COCKLE</data><data key="n_page">223</data><data key="n_scientific">Agrostemma githago L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">Homeopathic only (HH2; PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM; PHR). “Health hazards not known with proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). Seed toxic, 2–3 g considered harmless to humans, more than 5 g is potentially lethal (PH2). Signs of intoxication; colic, conjunctivosis, cramps, delirium, diarrhea, dizziness, headache, lacrimation, mucositis, and restlessness (PH2). LD50 (saponin mix) = 750 mg/kg orl mouse (HH2). LD50 (saponin mix) = 2.3 mg/kg ivn rat (HH2). LD50 (saponin mix) = 50 mg/kg orl rat (HH2). Sprouts contain allantoin.</data></node>
<node id="act:antimycotic"><data key="n_label">Antimycotic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 4 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:centaurea-cyanus-l-cornflower"><data key="n_label">Cornflower, Bachelor’S Button</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CORNFLOWER, BACHELOR’S BUTTON</data><data key="n_page">223</data><data key="n_scientific">Centaurea cyanus L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">1 g fl/cup (HHB); tea to be drunk several ×/day (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not recorded for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:papaver-rhoeas-l-corn-poppy"><data key="n_label">Corn Poppy</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CORN POPPY</data><data key="n_page">224</data><data key="n_scientific">Papaver rhoeas L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">1 g flower/cup tea (PH2); 2.5–5 ml flower syrup (PNC).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). Children have been poisoned eating the flowers and leaves (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="ind:cancer-heart"><data key="n_label">Cancer, heart</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:cancer-muscle"><data key="n_label">Cancer, muscle</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:myosarcoma"><data key="n_label">Myosarcoma</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:zea-mays-l-corn-silk"><data key="n_label">Corn Silk</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CORN SILK</data><data key="n_page">224</data><data key="n_scientific">Zea mays L.</data><data key="n_safety">+++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+++</data><data key="n_dosages">4–8 g silk as tea 3 ×/day (CAN); 1 tsp silk/cup water up to several ×/day (WIC); 2 tsp corn silk/cup tea every other day (PH2); 4–8 ml liquid extract; 5–15 ml tincture (1:5 in 25% alcohol) 3 ×/day (CAN); 2–3 tsp tincture/day (20 g corn silk/100 ml 20% ethanol to steep 5 days) (PH2); 8–15 ml syrup (CAN); 2–8 ml liquid corn silk extract (PNC).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). CAN reports dermatosis and allergenic reaction. Because of its uterine stimulant effect in vivo, its use in pregnancy and lactation is to be avoided (CAN). Because of its diuretic activity, could lead to hypokalemia (CAN). Allantoin may explain cell-proliferant and vulnerary activity (FAD). APA questions the diuretic activity (APA).</data></node>
<node id="act:cell-proliferant"><data key="n_label">Cell Proliferant</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:corydalis-ambigua-cham-schltdl-and-or-c-yanhusuo-corydalis"><data key="n_label">Corydalis</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">CORYDALIS</data><data key="n_page">225</data><data key="n_scientific">Corydalis ambigua Cham. &amp; Schltdl. and/or C. yanhusuo (Y. H. Chou &amp; Chun. C. Hsu) W. T. Wang</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">2–6 g dry root/day or 4–12 ml fluid extract (1:2) for most purposes; 5–10 g dry root/day or 10–20 ml fluid extract (1:2) for analgesia (KEB).</data><data key="n_extracts">Oral doses of 1–50 mg/kg gindarin, based on dl-tetrahydropalmatine, embryotoxic to rats in days 1–20 of pregnancy. Methanolic extracts and dehydrocorydaline inhibit aldose reductase (KEB). Tetrahydroberberin is strongly antiaggregant by inhibiting platelet aggregation (KEB).</data></node>
<node id="act:dopamineantagonist"><data key="n_label">DopamineAntagonist</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
