<node id="ind:psittacosis"><data key="n_label">Psittacosis</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:toxoplasmosis"><data key="n_label">Toxoplasmosis</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:trilisa-odoratissima-deer-tongue"><data key="n_label">Deer Tongue</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Carphephorus odoratissimus (J. F. Gmel.) H. J.-C. Hebert.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DEER TONGUE</data><data key="n_page">247</data><data key="n_scientific">Trilisa odoratissima (Walter ex J. F. Gmel.) Cass.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (KOM; PHR). Coumarins may trigger hemorrhage and liver damage (CRC). New evidence cited in Marle et al. (1987) indicates that coumarin is not mutagenic, carcinogenic, or teratogenic in rodents. Coumarin failed to produce any sensitization reactions in 25 human volunteers. It therefore seems likely that coumarin has little toxic potential for humans with normal liver function.</data></node>
<node id="ind:lymphedema"><data key="n_label">Lymphedema</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:cedrus-deodara-deodar-cedar"><data key="n_label">Deodar Cedar</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Cedrus deodora f. aurea (J. Nelson) Rehder, Pinus deodara Roxb. ex D. Don</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DEODAR CEDAR</data><data key="n_page">247</data><data key="n_scientific">Cedrus deodara (Roxb. ex D. Don) G. Don</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_extracts">One butanol fraction highly antiinflammatory in rats. Contains many inflammatory and antispasmodic compounds (e.g., allohimacholol, centdarol, himachalol, himadarol spasmolytic). Delta-7-dehydrodomatuic-acid; delta-10-dehydroepitodomatuic-acid, and 7-hydroxytodomatuic-acid have juvabional (juvenile hormonal) activity.</data></node>
<node id="act:juvabional"><data key="n_label">Juvabional</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:rauvolfia-tetraphylla-l-devil-pepper"><data key="n_label">Devil-Pepper</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: R. canescens L., R. heterophylla Roem. &amp; Schult., R. hirsuta Jacq.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DEVIL-PEPPER</data><data key="n_page">247</data><data key="n_scientific">Rauvolfia tetraphylla L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM; PHR). Has reportedly caused fatalities.</data></node>
<node id="hierba:harpagophytum-procumbens-devils-claw"><data key="n_label">Devil’S Claw</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Uncaria procumbens Burch.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DEVIL’S CLAW</data><data key="n_page">248</data><data key="n_scientific">Harpagophytum procumbens (Burch.) DC ex. Meisn.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">1 tsp chopped root/2 cups water, sipped through day (APA); 1.5–4.5(–10) g root (KOM; SHT; SKY); 6 g root/day (MAB); 1–2 tsp fresh root (PED); 0.5–1 g dry root (PED); 1 g dry root:5 ml alcohol/5 ml water (PED); 0.1–0.25 g powdered tuber (PNC); 0.1–0.25 g dry tuber as tea 3 ×/day (CAN); 0.1–0.25 ml liquid extract (1:1 in 25% ethanol) 3 ×/day (CAN); 6–12 ml liquid extract (1:2)/day (MAB); 15–30 ml tincture (1:5)/day (MAB); 0.5–1 ml root tincture (1:5 in 25% alcohol) 3 ×/day (CAN).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2b, 2d (AHP). Contraindicated in duodenal and gastric ulcers (AHP, 1997). Commission E reports contraindications in GI ulcer (AEH). Contraindicated in people with diabetes. Excessive doses may interfere with blood pressure and cardiac therapy (CAN). LD50 = &gt;13,500 mg/kg orl mouse (CAN).</data><data key="n_extracts">German clinical studies confirm arthritic relief; hypocholesterolemic, hypouricemic (PED). Chrubasik et al. (1996) studied the effectiveness in treatment of acute low back pain. While animal studies exhibit analgesic and antiinflammatory activities (due to harpagoside), this study of 118 patients with nonspecific low-back pain (most for more than 15 years), with 400 mg extract 3 ×/day (equivalent of 6000 mg crude root extract = 50 mg harpagoside). Only 9 of the treated patients improved cf 1 in the placebo controls. The insignificant reduction in pain was confined to those whose pain did not radiate to one or both legs. “There was a notable absence of identifiable clinical, hematological, or biochemical side effects” (PHM3:1). None of these authors commented on the presence of 3 COX-2 inhibitors as well, kaempferol, oleanolic acid, and ursolic acid.</data></node>
<node id="ind:cns"><data key="n_label">CNS</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:fibromyalgia"><data key="n_label">Fibromyalgia</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:digitalis-purpurea-l-digitalis"><data key="n_label">Digitalis</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DIGITALIS</data><data key="n_page">249</data><data key="n_scientific">Digitalis purpurea L.</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_dosages">Prescription only (JAD).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 3 (AHP). Inappropriate for obesity treatment (AHP). One source reports it is classified as a drug, which must normally be registered as pharmaceutical specialty (AEH). Why repeat; use with arrhythmogenic drugs (methylxanthines, phosphodiesterase inhibitors (including, I suppose, Viagra™), quinidine, sympathomimetics) increases risk of arrhythmia (PH2). Long-term dosage is potentially gynecomastic. Overdoses may cause anorexia, confusion, depressions, diarrhea, gastric hypertonia, hallucinogens, headache, psychosis, stupor, tachycardia, and vomiting. Lethal doses induce asphyxiation or heart failure (PH2). Both the leaf and pure glycosides are contraindicated in carotid sinus syndrome, first and second degree AV-block, hypercalcemia, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, hypokalemia, thoracic aortic aneurism, ventricular tachycardia, or WPW syndrome (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="act:vasculostimulant"><data key="n_label">Vasculostimulant</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:hydropsy"><data key="n_label">Hydropsy</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:anethum-graveolens-l-dill"><data key="n_label">Dill</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: A. sowa Roxb. ex Fleming, Peucedanum graveolens (L.) Benth. &amp; Hook f.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DILL</data><data key="n_page">250</data><data key="n_scientific">Anethum graveolens L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">Chew 1/2–1 tsp seed (APA); 2 tsp crushed seed/cup water (APA); 3 g seed (KOM; PHR); 0.3–1 ml concentrated dill water (PNC); 0.1–0.3 g EO (KOM; PHR); 0.05–2 ml (I’d have said 0.05–0.2) EO (PNC); cf celery seed, close kin (PNC).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP, 1997). None known (KOM). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). Contact photodermatosis possible as in most umbellifers. Fresh juice may possibly cause photodermatosis (PHR). We might extend to all apiaceous oils Bisset’s comments on celery seed oil, “The drug is contraindicated in inflammation of the kidneys,” since apiaceous EOs may increase the inflammation as a result of epithelial irritation (BIS). TRAMIL conservatively cautions for dill and fennel; contraindicated in women of reproductive age; limit dosage to 3 days (TRA).</data></node>
<node id="ind:shigellosis"><data key="n_label">Shigellosis</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:caesalpinia-bonduc-divi-divi"><data key="n_label">Divi-Divi</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Caesalpinia bonducella (L.) Fleming, C. crista auct. Amer., Guilandina bonduc L., G. bonducella L.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DIVI-DIVI</data><data key="n_page">251</data><data key="n_scientific">Caesalpinia bonduc (L.) Roxb.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">Up to 1 g mixed with pepper as tonic (HHB).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2).</data><data key="n_extracts">Fatty acid triglycerides (octadeca-4-enoic-, octadeca-2,4-dienoic-, palmitic-, and stearic-acids) are macrofilaricidal (from kernels of C. bonduc seed). Crude ethanol extracts were active against Litomosoides carinii in the cotton rat. Most or all activity was in the n-hexane soluble fraction. Orally, it killed nearly 95% of the microfilariae at a dose of 1 g/kg/×5 days (Rastogi et al., 1996).</data></node>
<node id="act:filaricide"><data key="n_label">Filaricide</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:cancer-scrotum"><data key="n_label">Cancer, scrotum</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 6 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:salvia-divinorum-epling-jativa-divining-sage"><data key="n_label">Divining Sage</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DIVINING SAGE</data><data key="n_page">252</data><data key="n_scientific">Salvia divinorum Epling &amp; Jativa</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM; PHR). Narcotic hallucinogen (CRC).</data></node>
<node id="act:telepathic"><data key="n_label">Telepathic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:cuscuta-epithymum-dodder"><data key="n_label">Dodder, Clover Dodder</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: C. europaea var. epithymum L.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DODDER, CLOVER DODDER</data><data key="n_page">252</data><data key="n_scientific">Cuscuta epithymum (L.) L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) Overdoses may cause colic (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:elaeophorbia-drupifera-dodo"><data key="n_label">Dodo, Kankan, Toro</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Euphorbia drupifera Thonn.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DODO, KANKAN, TORO</data><data key="n_page">252</data><data key="n_scientific">Elaeophorbia drupifera (Thonn.) Stapf</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_downsides">Classed as a narcotic hallucinogen (?). “The caustic latex can cause permanent blindness if introduced into the eyes” (CRC).</data></node>
<node id="act:caustic"><data key="n_label">Caustic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="act:filaricidal"><data key="n_label">Filaricidal</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:apocynum-cannabinum-l-dogbane"><data key="n_label">Dogbane, Indian Hemp</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: A. suksdorfii Greene.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DOGBANE, INDIAN HEMP</data><data key="n_page">252</data><data key="n_scientific">Apocynum cannabinum L.</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_dosages">10–30 drops liquid extract 3 ×/day; 0.3 to 0.6 ml tincture (1:10) (PHR; PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 3 (AHP). Cardiac glycosides (AHP). Nausea and GI symptoms may occur even at therapeutic dosage; topically may irritate mucous membranes (PHR). Cymarin and apocannoside have antitumor activity (CEB).</data></node>
<node id="ind:hydrocephalus"><data key="n_label">Hydrocephalus</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:pox"><data key="n_label">Pox</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:valvular-insufficiency"><data key="n_label">Valvular Insufficiency</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:cornus-florida-l-dogwood"><data key="n_label">Dogwood</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DOGWOOD</data><data key="n_page">253</data><data key="n_scientific">Cornus florida L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">2–4 g powdered bark (PNC); 2–4 ml liquid extract (PNC).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not recorded for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). Warning: As with hard toothbrushes, chewing sticks of dogwood can cause receding gums (FAD).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:houttuynia-cordata-thunb-dokudami"><data key="n_label">Dokudami, Chamaleon Plant</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DOKUDAMI, CHAMALEON PLANT</data><data key="n_page">253</data><data key="n_scientific">Houttuynia cordata Thunb.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">Food farmacy (JAD); 10–15 g in decoction (HHB); 15–30 g (FAY).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM; PH2). May cause dyspnea (shortness of breath) if overconsumed (LMP; TAD).</data></node>
<node id="act:osteogenic"><data key="n_label">Osteogenic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="act:prostaglandin-synthase-inhibitor"><data key="n_label">Prostaglandin-Synthase Inhibitor</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:leucorrhea"><data key="n_label">Leucorrhea</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:phagocytotic"><data key="n_label">Phagocytotic</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:angelica-sinensis-dong-quai"><data key="n_label">Dong Quai</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Angelica polymorpha var. sinensis Oliv.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DONG QUAI</data><data key="n_page">254</data><data key="n_scientific">Angelica sinensis (Oliv.) Diels</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">4.5–12 g (FAY); 3–15 g root (APA); 3–4 g root/day (SKY); 1–3 tsp root/day; 1–3 tbsp fresh root (PED); 2–4 g dry root (PED); 3–15 g dry root/day (MAB); 2–6 g dry root/day or 4–12 ml fluid extract (1:2) (for all his Chinese posologies, these ratios apply) (KEB); 3 g dry root:15 ml alcohol/15 ml water (PED); 4–8 ml flower extract (1:2) (MAB); 3 (530 mg) capsules 3 ×/day (NH).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2b (AHP). Psoralens can be phototoxic and carcinogenic. The aqueous extract inhibits experimentally induced IgE titers, suggesting immunosuppressive potential. “Its use cannot be recommended” (LRNP, April 1990). Pregnant and nursing women, or patients with diarrhea, should not take except under a doctor’s instruction. AHP notes that it is Americans and Canadians, not Chinese, who warn against use in pregnancy. Contraindicated in those with bleeding tendencies or heavy menstrual periods, during first trimester of pregnancy, for abortion-prone women, and in acute viral infections (KEB). “Canadian regulations do not allow dong-quai as a non-medicinal ingredient for oral use products” (Michols, 1995).</data><data key="n_extracts">Decoction uterocontractant in dogs. Decoction and tinctures hypotensive in anesthetized animals (PED). Ligustilide at 450 mg/day helped 77% of dysmenorrheics as opposed to 38% for aqueous extracts of the herb (KEB). Butylidenephthalide, butylphthalide, and ligustilide antispasmodic (FAY). Treatment of infertile women by uterine lavage of the extract for up to 9 months restored tubal patency in 79%; 53% became pregnant. Almost nontoxic LD50 = 100,000 mg/kg (MAB).</data></node>
<node id="act:female-tonic"><data key="n_label">Female Tonic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
