<node id="hierba:pseudotsuga-menziesii-douglas-fir"><data key="n_label">Douglas Fir</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DOUGLAS FIR</data><data key="n_page">255</data><data key="n_scientific">Pseudotsuga menziesii (Mirb.) Franco.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data></node>
<node id="act:antidote-cicuta"><data key="n_label">Antidote, cicuta</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:croton-lechleri-mull-arg-dragons-blood"><data key="n_label">Dragon’S Blood</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DRAGON’S BLOOD</data><data key="n_page">256</data><data key="n_scientific">Croton lechleri Müll. Arg.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">Pisco suggests a couple drops of the “blood” in a glass of water for topical and internal applications. Taspine, pycnogenol™, and dimethylcedrusine alone speed up healing two-fold on exfoliated rodents, but the whole dragon’s blood does it four times better (ABS).</data></node>
<node id="ind:rsv"><data key="n_label">RSV</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:daemonorops-draco-dragons-blood-palm"><data key="n_label">Dragon’S Blood Palm</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Calamus draco Willd.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DRAGON’S BLOOD PALM</data><data key="n_page">256</data><data key="n_scientific">Daemonorops draco (Willd.) Blume</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:drosera-rotundifolia-l-drosera"><data key="n_label">Drosera, Round-Leaved Sundew</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DROSERA, ROUND-LEAVED SUNDEW</data><data key="n_page">257</data><data key="n_scientific">Drosera rotundifolia L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">1–2 g dry leaf as tea 3 ×/day (CAN); 0.5–2 ml liquid leaf extract (1:1 in 25% ethanol) 3 ×/day (CAN); 0.5–1.0 ml herb tincture (1:5 in 60% alcohol) 3 ×/day (CAN); 3 g/day (KOM; PIP); steep 1–2 drug 10 min. in 1 cup boiled water, 3–4 ×/day (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2b, 2c (AHP; CAN). No contraindications documented. None reported (KOM; PIP). Plumbagin may be irritant (CAN). Plumbagin is immunostimulating in small doses, antibacterial, antifungal, antileishmanic, and antiviral as well as cytotoxic in large doses (PNC).</data></node>
<node id="ind:old-age"><data key="n_label">Old Age</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:lemna-minor-l-duckweed"><data key="n_label">Duckweed</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DUCKWEED</data><data key="n_page">257</data><data key="n_scientific">Lemna minor L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">One tsp fresh plant (MAD). Homeopathic (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">“Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) (but PH2 designates only a homeopathic dosage! JAD).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:dieffenbachia-seguine-dumbcane"><data key="n_label">Dumbcane</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Arum seguine Jacq., Caladium maculatum G. Lodd., Dieffenbachia amoena hort., D. baraquiniana Verschaff. &amp; Lem., D. exotica hort., D. maculata (G. Lodd.) G. Don, D. picta Schott, D. picta var. baraquiniana (Verschaff. &amp; Lem.) Engl.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DUMBCANE</data><data key="n_page">258</data><data key="n_scientific">Dieffenbachia seguine (Jacq.) Schott</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_downsides">“Acicular crystals of calcium oxalate, accompanied by a protein (enzyme) or asparagine, may cause severe burning in the mouth and throat. Swelling of the mouth may be severe enough to cause fatal choking. The sap, under some conditions, is an irritant and vesicant. Contact with bruised plants seemed to be necessary for irritation. Panama natives, to this day, blame an indolent ulcer I suffered on the ankle on my walking through a patch of dumbcane that had recently been cut. On several occasions I applied fresh latex to my wrist with no problem.” (CRC).</data></node>
<node id="act:cyanogenetic"><data key="n_label">Cyanogenetic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:yaw"><data key="n_label">Yaw</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 5 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:senecio-cineraria-dc-dusty-miller"><data key="n_label">Dusty-Miller</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Senecio bicolor (Willd.) Tod.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DUSTY-MILLER</data><data key="n_page">259</data><data key="n_scientific">Senecio cineraria DC.</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_dosages">Don’t take it (JAD, PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). Contains pyrrolizidine alkaloids (PAs) in considerable amounts and varieties, which can be carcinogenic, hepatotoxic, and mutagenic. PAs are toxic to humans. Use of Senecio species can result in potentially fatal hepatic veno-occlusive disease in humans, with symptoms of abdominal pain and vomiting with ascites, or seneciosis (VOD), reported in almost all cases of severe or fatal intoxications, from intakes of 0.5 mg/kg to 3.3 mg/kg (AEH1).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:dicentra-cucullaria-dutchmans-breeches"><data key="n_label">Dutchman’S Breeches, Turkey Corn</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Fumaria cucullaria L.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DUTCHMAN’S BREECHES, TURKEY CORN</data><data key="n_page">259</data><data key="n_scientific">Dicentra cucullaria (L.) Bernh.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM). None recorded at proper dosage (not stated) (PH2). Bicuculline could cause poisoning in high doses (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="act:gabaantagonist"><data key="n_label">GABAAntagonist</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:eucommia-ulmoides-oliv-du-zhong"><data key="n_label">Du Zhong, Gutta-Percha Tree</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">DU ZHONG, GUTTA-PERCHA TREE</data><data key="n_page">260</data><data key="n_scientific">Eucommia ulmoides Oliv.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">6–15 g bark, in decoction, pill, powder, or tincture (FAY); 30 drops 10% tincture 3 ×/day, ca 9 months, for high blood pressure (FAY).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP). Not covered (KOM; PH2).</data><data key="n_extracts">Quercetin (at 8.5 µM) was most potent of 5-alpha-glucosidase inhibitors found in extracts of the leaves (X9028049). Leaves contain several anticlastogenic (antimutagenic) compounds (asperulosidic acid, deacetyl asperulosidic acid, asperuloside, geniposidic acid, geniposide, p-trans-coumaric acid pyrogallol, and protocatechuic acid) (X9025787). At 100 µg/ml, the leaf extract was more potent a radical scavenger than isolated protocatechuic acid at the same concentration, clearly a super example of synergy (X10956129). With at least six compounds that tend to stimulate collagen production, this herb certainly has antiaging potential (including antiwrinkle activity). Scientists favor one part ginseng:four parts du zhong for synergistically stimulating collagen production (X9084879).</data></node>
<node id="act:anticlastogenic"><data key="n_label">Anticlastogenic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="act:antiperoxidant"><data key="n_label">Antiperoxidant</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="act:granulomagenic"><data key="n_label">Granulomagenic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
