<node id="ind:cancer-pudenda"><data key="n_label">Cancer, pudenda</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:paeonia-officinalis-l-european-peony"><data key="n_label">European Peony</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">EUROPEAN PEONY</data><data key="n_page">284</data><data key="n_scientific">Paeonia officinalis L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">1 tsp (~1 g) flower/cup water (APA; PH2; WIC); 30–40 grains seed for nightmare (FEL); 2 g seed (HHB); 3–12 g peony root (FAY); 1 oz root boiled in a pint of liquid (1 part “good gin”:2 parts water) (FEL); 1–30 drops root tincture (FEL); 0.3–0.6 g powder root for children (MAD); 5–15 drops tincture (MAD); 2–3 fluid oz 3–4 ×/day (FEL).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1. “Canadian regulations do not allow European peony as a nonmedicinal ingredient for oral use products” (AHP). No peonies were approved by Commission E, but they did say, “No risks known” (KOM). The Physicians Desk Reference was a bit more explicit regarding P. officinalis, “Health hazards not known with proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). “Side effects that may occur, particularly in cases of over dosages, include gastroenterosis with vomiting, colic, and diarrhea” (PHR). “Because efficacy has not been documented, therapeutic use cannot be recommended” (PH2). Much of the indicated uses and biological activity is attributed to paeoniflorin.</data></node>
<node id="act:hypertonic"><data key="n_label">Hypertonic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
