<node id="ind:splinter"><data key="n_label">Splinter</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:anamirta-cocculus-fish-berry"><data key="n_label">Fish Berry, Levant Nut</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Anamirta paniculata Colebr., Menispermum cocculus L.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">FISH BERRY, LEVANT NUT</data><data key="n_page">302</data><data key="n_scientific">Anamirta cocculus (L.) Wight &amp; Arn.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">Up to two fruits (HH2); do not take without responsible dispenser (JAD); 1–5 mg picrotoxin ivn; or 1 mg suppositories for 3 weeks (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM). Very poisonous, causing convulsions, delirium, depression, diaphoresis, disturbed coordination, dizziness, dyspnea, headache, nausea, and spastic twitching (PH2; WOI; WO2). 2–3 g fruit can kill a human (HH2); 2.4 g have killed a person (WOI).</data></node>
<node id="act:antidote-barbituate"><data key="n_label">Antidote, barbituate</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="act:antidote-morphine"><data key="n_label">Antidote, morphine</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:nystagmus"><data key="n_label">Nystagmus</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:vitex-negundo-l-five-leaved-chaste-tree"><data key="n_label">Five-Leaved Chaste Tree, Huang Jing</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">FIVE-LEAVED CHASTE TREE, HUANG JING</data><data key="n_page">303</data><data key="n_scientific">Vitex negundo L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">3–9 fruit in decoction; 15–30 g as powder; 15–60 g fresh leaf; 3–9 g powdered dry leaf (FAY); 6–12 g root (FAY); 3–6 g branches in decoction (FAY); 9 g powdered seed 3 ×/day for 9 days for bronchitis (FAY).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM; PH2). With potential or adverse cardiac activity due to cardiac glycosides, plant should be used with caution in patients with heart problems (FAY).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:sida-cordifolia-l-flannelweed"><data key="n_label">Flannelweed, Country Mallow</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">FLANNELWEED, COUNTRY MALLOW</data><data key="n_page">303</data><data key="n_scientific">Sida cordifolia L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">1–3 g powder (KAP); 2–20 ml root tea (KAP).</data><data key="n_extracts">HDN presents new pharmacological data; some of it conflicts with what I provide. For example, in HDN, vasicinone was reported as a bronchoconstrictor in vivo and as a bronchodilator in vitro. HDN gives us the activities for L-ephedrine, while not specifically naming it in the plant. Beta-phenylethylamine is the parent of many different sympathomimetic phytochemicals. Well absorbed, it easily crosses the blood-brain barrier, decreasing amine content in noradrenalin and dopamine-containing nerve terminals in the brain and in the peripheral adrenergic nerve terminals of rats (HDN). Its amphetamine-like action can be blocked by pretreatment with reserpine (HDN).</data></node>
<node id="act:teratogen"><data key="n_label">Teratogen</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:spondylosis"><data key="n_label">Spondylosis</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:linum-usitatissimum-l-flax"><data key="n_label">Flax</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Linum crepitans (Boenn.) Dumort., L. humile Mill., L. usitatissimum subsp. transitorium Vavilov &amp; Elladi, L. usitatissimum var. humile (Mill.) Pers.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">FLAX</data><data key="n_page">305</data><data key="n_scientific">Linum usitatissimum L.</data><data key="n_safety">+++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+++</data><data key="n_dosages">1 (1300 mg) StX for 740 mg ALA, softgel (APA); 1 tbsp whole or crushed (not ground) seed/150 ml liquid 2–3 ×/day (APA; PH2; PIP); 1 oz seed/1 quart water at boiling point (FEL); 35–50 g crushed seed/day for hypercholesterolemia (PH2); 1–2 tbsp flaxseed oil/day as antiaggregant (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2d. Contraindicated in bowel obstruction (AHP). May lead to bowel obstruction if fluid intake is inadequate. “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) (but PH2 designates no specific quantified dosage! JAD). May adversely affect absorption of drugs (as with any mucilaginous preparation). Commission E reports contraindications: ileus; interactions: reduced absorption of other drugs possible. No adverse effects when used with a sufficient amount of liquid. Other sources report contraindications: intestinal obstruction; patients with inflammatory intestinal diseases should use the seed only in its swollen state. Abuse of high doses may result in electrolyte losses (AEH). Bulking agents should not be taken by those with stenotic lesions of the GI tract. Do not take bulking agents when lying down or at bedtime. Do not use with antiperistaltics (like, e.g., loperamide) (SHT). Do not use seed in patients with diverticulitis (WAM). Under pessimal conditions, 100 g linseed can liberate up to 50 mg HCN, enough to bring about symptoms of poisoning. But these conditions, rarely, if ever are maintained. Most HCN is metabolized via the enzyme rhodanase, which can convert 30–60 mg HCN per hour into the relatively toxic thiocyanate. Even doses of 300 g of ground linseed evoked no symptoms of poisoning in a volunteer. Single doses of 100 g reported to not cause significant rise in blood HCN and thiocyanate levels. Taking 15 g linseed thrice daily for 3 to 4 weeks raised thiocyanate levels in blood and urine (Bisset, 1994). Don’t confuse flaxseed oil with more poisonous denatured linseed oil (WAM). Seeds, like those of Perilla, are one of the best sources of that omega-3 fatty acid called ALA (alpha-linolenic acid). See Rudin’s, The Omega-3 Phenomenon, for an incredible list of supposed indications for flax seed oil, an entire book full.</data></node>
<node id="ind:ascarides"><data key="n_label">Ascarides</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:lumbricus"><data key="n_label">Lumbricus</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:lymphangosis"><data key="n_label">Lymphangosis</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:tiarella-cordifolia-l-foam-flower"><data key="n_label">Foam-Flower, Coolwort</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">FOAM-FLOWER, COOLWORT</data><data key="n_page">306</data><data key="n_scientific">Tiarella cordifolia L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">1 oz herb/pint infusion; take 4 oz tea 2–3 ×/day (GMH).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) (but PH2 designates no specific quantified dosage! JAD).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:aethusa-cynapium-l-fools-parsley"><data key="n_label">Fool’S Parsley</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">FOOL’S PARSLEY</data><data key="n_page">307</data><data key="n_scientific">Aethusa cynapium L.</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_downsides">Toxic; human fatalities reported (PHR). Sometimes fatally confused with parsley (CRC). Poisonous, but less so than poison hemlock (PH2). Aethusanol-A: LD50 = 100.8 mg/kg ipr mouse (HH2), aethusin: LD50 = 93.3 mg/kg ipr mouse (HH2).</data></node>
<node id="ind:lactose-intolerance"><data key="n_label">Lactose Intolerance</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:pylorosis"><data key="n_label">Pylorosis</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:myosotis-arvensis-forget-me-not"><data key="n_label">Forget-Me-Not</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: M. scorpioides var. arvensis L.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">FORGET-ME-NOT</data><data key="n_page">307</data><data key="n_scientific">Myosotis arvensis (L.) Hill.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">0.5 tsp herb 5 ×/day in tea (MAD).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM). Should not be taken because it contains pyrrolizidine alkaloids (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:forsythia-suspensa-forsythia"><data key="n_label">Forsythia, Golden Bells</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Syringa suspensa Thunb.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">FORSYTHIA, GOLDEN BELLS</data><data key="n_page">307</data><data key="n_scientific">Forsythia suspensa (Thunb.) Vahl</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">6–15 g fruit (FAY); 6–9 g stem and/or leaf (FAY).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2b (AHP). Emmenagogue and uterotonic (AHP). Not covered (KOM; PH2).</data></node>
<node id="act:anti-itch"><data key="n_label">Anti-itch</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="act:antinauseant"><data key="n_label">Antinauseant</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:polygonum-multiflorum-thunb-fo-ti"><data key="n_label">Fo-Ti</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">FO-TI</data><data key="n_page">308</data><data key="n_scientific">Polygonum multiflorum Thunb.</data><data key="n_safety">+++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+++</data><data key="n_dosages">6–15 g dry root (LAF); 4–5 g dry root or 8–16 ml fluid extract (1:2) (KEB); 0.5–1 dropper concentrated root extract 2–3 ×/day (APA); 3–5 g/cup water 3 ×/day (SKY); 5 (500 mg) tablets 3 ×/day (FAY; SKY); 2–3 (560 mg) capsules 3 ×/day (NH).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2d. Raw root is laxative; prepared root may cause GI distress; contraindicated with diarrhea (AHP). May cause colic, diarrhea, nausea, numbness of the extremities, and skin rashes (AHP; SKY). Allergic cystosis may result (LAF). Emodin has vasodilator and immunosuppressive effects in test tubes. LD50 of alcoholic percolates 169 to 2700 mg/kg (raw to cured) (LAF).</data></node>
<node id="act:antiprogestational"><data key="n_label">Antiprogestational</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
