<node id="ind:cancer-teat"><data key="n_label">Cancer, teat</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:abies-fraseri-frasers-fir"><data key="n_label">Fraser’S Fir, She Balsam</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">FRASER’S FIR, SHE BALSAM</data><data key="n_page">312</data><data key="n_scientific">Abies fraseri (Pursh) Poir.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data></node>
<node id="hierba:chionanthus-virginicus-l-fringetree"><data key="n_label">Fringetree</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">FRINGETREE</data><data key="n_page">312</data><data key="n_scientific">Chionanthus virginicus L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">0.3–1.5 ml liquid root bark extract (PNC).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP). None noted (PHR). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (not given) (PH2). Overdoses may cause frontal headache, slow pulse, vomiting (FAD).</data></node>
<node id="act:hepatic"><data key="n_label">Hepatic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:bilious-fever"><data key="n_label">Bilious Fever</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:cunila-origanoides-frost-mint"><data key="n_label">Frost Mint</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Cunila mariana L., Satureja origanoides L.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">FROST MINT</data><data key="n_page">313</data><data key="n_scientific">Cunila origanoides (L.) Britton</data><data key="n_safety">+++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+++</data><data key="n_dosages">Probably on par with thyme, culinarily and medicinally (i.e., 1 tsp herb/cup water 1–3 ×/day; 1–4 g dry herb, or in tea, 3 ×/day; 1–2 g/cup several ×/day).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM; PH2). I feel it as safe as thyme and oregano, based on the limited list of chemicals available to me.</data></node>
<node id="hierba:helianthemum-canadense-frostwort"><data key="n_label">Frostwort</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">FROSTWORT</data><data key="n_page">313</data><data key="n_scientific">Helianthemum canadense (L.) Michx.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not recorded for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) (but PH2 designates no specific quantified dosage! JAD).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:fucus-vesiculosus-l-fucus"><data key="n_label">Fucus, Bladderwrack</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">FUCUS, BLADDERWRACK</data><data key="n_page">313</data><data key="n_scientific">Fucus vesiculosus L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">5–10 g alga 3 ×/day (CAN); 0.25–0.5 cup fresh alga (PED); 2–4 tsp dry alga (PED); 3 tsp dry alga:75 ml alcohol/75 ml water (PED); 4–8 ml liquid algal extract (1:1 in 25% ethanol) 3 ×/day (CAN); 3.6–7.3 g 4–5 ×/day (MAD).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2b, 2c, 2d. Not recommended for long-term use, especially in hyperthyroids (AHP). Commission E reports thallus not approved for therapeutic use. Other sources report thallus permitted as laxative. Usefulness is not documented adequately. There are no risks from daily doses up to 150 µg iodine per day, but higher doses may induce or exacerbate hyperthyreosis and cause hypersensitivity reactions (rarely) (PHR). The adult intake of iodine should not exceed 120 µg per day (AEH). Iodine content may cause hyperthyroidism or hypothyroidism (I suspect that there are also cases where it can help one and the other) (CAN). Because of thyroid gland activity and possible heavy metal contamination, its use in pregnancy and lactation is to be avoided (CAN). One fraction is half as antiaggregant as heparin. High in vitro activity to mammalian digestive enzymes (amylase, lipase, and trypsin). Lectin-like mucopolysaccharides modulate the immune system, inducing lymphocyte transformation. Alginic acid is a laxative (CAN).</data></node>
<node id="act:antihypothyroid"><data key="n_label">Antihypothyroid</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="act:hemagglutinant"><data key="n_label">Hemagglutinant</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:fumaria-officinalis-l-fumitory"><data key="n_label">Fumitory</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">FUMITORY</data><data key="n_page">314</data><data key="n_scientific">Fumaria officinalis L.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">1.25–2 tsp (2–3 g) herb/cup water up to 2 ×/day (APA); 2–3 tsp (2.4–3.6 g) herb/cup tea/day (MAD); 2–4 g herb, or in tea, 3 ×/day (CAN); 6 g herb/day, drink 1 cup warm tea (2–3 g/cup, steeped 20 minutes) 30 minutes before meals (PH2); 6 g/day (APA; KOM; SHT); 2–4 ml liquid herb extract (1:1 in 25% ethanol) 3 ×/day (CAN); 2–4 ml liquid herb extract (PNC); 2–4 g aqueous extract (HHB); 1–4 ml herb tincture (1:5 in 45% ethanol) 3 ×/day (CAN); 3.65 g sap/day (MAD); 2–3 tsp (2.4–3.5 g) expressed juice in hot or cold infusion (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not listed (AHP). None reported (KOM). HH3 says protopine is spasmogenic; I’m more inclined to believe BRU, which calls it antispasmodic.</data></node>
<node id="act:amphicholeretic"><data key="n_label">Amphicholeretic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:bile-duct-spasm"><data key="n_label">Bile-Duct Spasm</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:kaempferia-galanga-l-galanga"><data key="n_label">Galanga</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">GALANGA</data><data key="n_page">315</data><data key="n_scientific">Kaempferia galanga L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1, but giving it the same common name as Alpinia galanga, greater galangal (AHP). Not covered, at least under the generic name Kaempferia (EFS; KOM; PH2).</data></node>
<node id="ind:lameness"><data key="n_label">Lameness</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 6 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:ferula-gummosa-boiss-galbanum"><data key="n_label">Galbanum</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Ferula galbaniflua Boiss. &amp; Buhse.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">GALBANUM</data><data key="n_page">316</data><data key="n_scientific">Ferula gummosa Boiss.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data></node>
<node id="ind:gatrosis"><data key="n_label">Gatrosis</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:scleroma"><data key="n_label">Scleroma</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 4 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:uncaria-gambir-gambir"><data key="n_label">Gambir, Pale Catechu</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Nauclea gambir W. Hunter, Ourouparia gambir (W. Hunter) Baill. Some GMH data apply more appropriately to Acacia catechu; both are colloquially called catechu, or Uncaria pale catechu. The tanniniferous resins of the two are used interchangeably, thanks to their high tannin content.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">GAMBIR, PALE CATECHU</data><data key="n_page">316</data><data key="n_scientific">Uncaria gambir (W. Hunter) Roxb.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">0.3–1 g resin (HHB; HH3); 0.5–2 g drug (PH2); 2.5–5 ml catechu tincture (200 g pounded herb, 50 g cut cinnamon to 1 liter 45% ethanol) (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP).“Health hazards not known with proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:garcinia-hanburyi-hook-f-gamboge"><data key="n_label">Gamboge</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">GAMBOGE</data><data key="n_page">317</data><data key="n_scientific">Garcinia hanburyi Hook. f.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">0.1 g resin (HHB).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). As little as 200 mg resin can induce colic and nausea (PHR). Fatalities with 4 g (PHR; PH2). Extracts bactericidal and protisticidal against, for example, Aerobacter, Enterovirus, Micrococcus, and Mycobacteria (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:lepidium-sativum-l-garden-cress"><data key="n_label">Garden Cress</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">GARDEN CRESS</data><data key="n_page">317</data><data key="n_scientific">Lepidium sativum L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">1–2.5 drachms seed (DEP); 1–3 fluid oz decoction 3–4 ×/day (DEP); none given (PHR).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) (but PH2 designates no specific quantified dosage! JAD). Large doses can irritate GI mucous membranes (PHR). Mustard oil can cause blisters and necrosis (PH2). Seed extracts are possibly teratological (ZUL). I decided against accepting the Herbal PDR (PH2) report of 37% ascorbic acid, although I have seen reports almost that high for other better known sources of vitamin C.</data></node>
