<node id="ind:urinary-discharge"><data key="n_label">Urinary Discharge</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:aegopodium-podagraria-l-goutweed"><data key="n_label">Goutweed</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">GOUTWEED</data><data key="n_page">346</data><data key="n_scientific">Aegopodium podagraria L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">30–60 ml fresh plant juice (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; PH2).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:aframomum-melegueta-k-schum-grains-of-paradise"><data key="n_label">Grains-Of-Paradise</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: Amomum melegueta Roscoe.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">GRAINS-OF-PARADISE</data><data key="n_page">346</data><data key="n_scientific">Aframomum melegueta K. Schum.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP, KOM). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) (but PH2 designates no specific quantified dosage! Nor do I (JAD)). Pungent principles may irritate gastric and urinary tracts (PH2). High oxalic content of the seed may induce cardiopathy (WO2). Antimalarial tests proved negative (UPW). Abreu and Noronha (1997) remind us that the pungent (spicy) principles have antifeedant, antischistosomal, antiseptic, antitermite, and molluscicidal properties.</data></node>
<node id="act:antischistosomal"><data key="n_label">Antischistosomal</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="act:antitermite"><data key="n_label">Antitermite</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:passiflora-quadrangularis-l-granadilla"><data key="n_label">Granadilla</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_description">Sinónimos: P. macrocarpa Mast., P. quadrangularis var. variegata.</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">GRANADILLA</data><data key="n_page">346</data><data key="n_scientific">Passiflora quadrangularis L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_downsides">Contains noradrenalin (DAV).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:vitis-vinifera-l-grape"><data key="n_label">Grape</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">GRAPE</data><data key="n_page">347</data><data key="n_scientific">Vitis vinifera L.</data><data key="n_safety">+++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+++</data><data key="n_dosages">75–600 mg seed extract (or pycnogenol)/day for up to 3 weeks; maintenance dose 40–100 mg/day (APA, PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">None yet known. Not in (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2).</data><data key="n_extracts">In general, agricultural selection breeds out some of the natural pesticides, such as resveratrol, which also have many interesting biological activities. The recent ACS book, Wine, Nutritional and Therapeutic Benefits, heaps praise on resveratrol, failing to tell us that there is 10–100 times more in the leaves, and I suspect seeds, than in the fruit pulp and wines. The seeds have only recently come to the market and clinical trials are few and far between. On the other hand, the fruits contain more than 30 types of anthocyanins. Small wonder that grapejuice has four times the ORAC score of any other fruit juice studied (JNU).</data></node>
<node id="act:antialopecic"><data key="n_label">Antialopecic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="act:antierythemic"><data key="n_label">Antierythemic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 4 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
