<node id="ind:viral-hepatosis"><data key="n_label">Viral Hepatosis</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:fallopia-japonica-hu-zhang"><data key="n_label">Hu-Zhang, Mexican Bamboo</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">HU-ZHANG, MEXICAN BAMBOO</data><data key="n_page">396</data><data key="n_scientific">Fallopia japonica (Houtt.) Ronse Decr.</data><data key="n_safety">+++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+++</data><data key="n_dosages">Having eaten the young shoots, which I consider food farmacy, and would not hesitate myself to take this at levels suggested for Fo Ti: 4–5(–16) g dry root or 8–16 (–40 ml fluid extract).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM; PH2). As the best source of resveratrol, a COX-2 inhibitor, this herb shares many of the activities of the so-called miracle aspirin. See also Fo-Ti, which shares many chemicals with Hu-Zhang.</data></node>
<node id="act:ptk-inhibitor"><data key="n_label">PTK-Inhibitor</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
