<node id="hierba:ceanothus-americanus-l-new-jersey-tea"><data key="n_label">New Jersey Tea</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">NEW JERSEY TEA</data><data key="n_page">526</data><data key="n_scientific">Ceanothus americanus L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">0.05–1.5 ml liquid extract (PNC).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP). None known (PHR). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). The 8% tannin could explain about half of the indications.</data></node>
<node id="hierba:alchornea-floribunda-muell-arg-niando"><data key="n_label">Niando, Iporuru</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">NIANDO, IPORURU</data><data key="n_page">527</data><data key="n_scientific">Alchornea floribunda Muell. Arg.</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM). Drug considered highly toxic, high doses causing excitation and cramps. In Africa “deaths through exhaustion have been observed among humans following over-stimulation and hallucination” (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="act:ganglioplegic"><data key="n_label">Ganglioplegic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="act:vagolytic"><data key="n_label">Vagolytic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="hierba:melaleuca-viridiflora-sol-ex-gaertn-niauli"><data key="n_label">Niauli</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">NIAULI</data><data key="n_page">527</data><data key="n_scientific">Melaleuca viridiflora Sol. ex Gaertn.</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">Internal: single dose 0.2 g oil, daily dose 0.2–2 g (KOM); nose drops (2–5% in vegetable oil) (KOM); external: 10–30% in oil (KOM).</data><data key="n_downsides">Blumenthal et al. (1998) and Fleming et al. (1998) are rougher on this one than the cajuput (and of course they did not even index or cover tea tree). For that reason, I score it only + for safety. Fleming et al. (1998) after issuing their usual template, which suggests that no health hazards or side effects have been noted with proper administration (no internal dosage defined) (PHR). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) (but PH2 designates no specific quantified dosage! JAD). Contraindicated internally in gallduct or GI or biliary inflammations, or severe liver ailments. Internal administration of niauli oil may lead to diarrhea, nausea, and vomiting. Do not apply near nostrils of pediatrics, may cause asthma-like attacks, bronchial spasm, glottal spasms, and even respiratory failure (KOM; PHR). Confusingly calling it caje rather than niauli oil, and noting that it contains 35–60% cineole, Fleming et al. say that cineole causes induction of enzymes involved in liver detoxification, thereby possibly shortening or lessening the effects of other drugs that might have been coadministered. (I suppose we can say that about all aromatic plants that contain significant quantities of cineole, and many do; what level of cineole is significant?) As with most EOs, this one may induce dermatosis in sensitive individuals. Fleming et al. even warn that overdosages (more than 10 g), can lead to life threatening poisonings, due to the cineole. Ten grams of niauli oil could contain 6 g cineole. Symptoms include circulatory disorders, collapse, fall in blood pressure, and respiratory failure. Do not induce vomiting, say Fleming et al., rather give activated charcoal (PHR).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:selenicereus-grandiflorus-night-blooming-cereus"><data key="n_label">Night-Blooming Cereus</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">NIGHT-BLOOMING CEREUS</data><data key="n_page">528</data><data key="n_scientific">Selenicereus grandiflorus (L.) Britt. &amp; Rose</data><data key="n_safety">+</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+</data><data key="n_dosages">0.6 ml fluid extract to 10 ×/day; 0.12 to 2 ml 2–3 ×/day (HH2; PHR; PH2); 10 drops tincture (1:10) in sweet water 3–5 ×/day (HH2; PHR).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 1 (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). The fresh juice can irritate the GI tract (CAN). Cactine may possibly have cardiotonic effects (PNC).</data></node>
<node id="act:spinostimulant"><data key="n_label">Spinostimulant</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 3 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
<node id="ind:prostate"><data key="n_label">Prostate</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 2 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:morinda-citrifolia-l-noni"><data key="n_label">Noni, Indian Mulberry</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">NONI, INDIAN MULBERRY</data><data key="n_page">529</data><data key="n_scientific">Morinda citrifolia L.</data><data key="n_safety">+++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">+++</data><data key="n_dosages">2–4 tbsp (COM = Commercial); PH2 says the fruits are inedible; ripe fruits, which smell of Roquefort cheese, are said to be a great favorite with Burmese (DEP); I side with the latter, the stinking fruits are widely considered edible among rural peoples.</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP; KOM; PHR). “Health hazards not known with proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2) (but PH2 designates no specific quantified dosage! JAD). I could do it the lazy way and just say, “None reported.” And since it is a food species, I could live with this. TRAMIL notes that fruits and leaves are edible. Tests for uterotonicity were negative. Leaf EO (of Morinda lucida) kills aflatoxin fungi at 1000 ppm.</data></node>
<node id="ind:sapremia"><data key="n_label">Sapremia</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:adiantum-pedatum-l-northern-maidenhair"><data key="n_label">Northern Maidenhair, Pointer Weed</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">NORTHERN MAIDENHAIR, POINTER WEED</data><data key="n_page">529</data><data key="n_scientific">Adiantum pedatum L.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">1.5 g herb/cup tea (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). Not for use during pregnancy (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="hierba:myristica-fragrans-houtt-nutmeg"><data key="n_label">Nutmeg</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">NUTMEG</data><data key="n_page">530</data><data key="n_scientific">Myristica fragrans Houtt.</data><data key="n_safety">++</data><data key="n_safety_raw">++</data><data key="n_dosages">0.3–1 g powdered nutmeg (APA; PNC); 0.05–0.2 ml EO (APA; PNC); 5–20 grains nutmeg (FEL); 300–600 mg 5–10 ×/day (HHB); 1–3 drops EO, 2–3 ×/day (PH2); 2–10 ml tincture/day (PH2); 0.3–1 g powdered nutmeg (PH2).</data><data key="n_downsides">Class 2b. Contains safrole. May interact with MAO. CNS-active (AHP). “Hazards and/or side effects not known for proper therapeutic dosages” (PH2). Not to be used during pregnancy. Can trigger allergic dermatitis (PH2). More than 5 g powdered nutmeg or mace can cause acute panic, anxiety, coma, dizziness, double vision, drowsiness, excessive thirst, hallucinations, headache, liver pain, nausea, stomach pain, even death (AHP). “... as little as 2 whole nutmegs have been known to cause death in a little boy” (APA; FEL). Commission E reports contraindications for seed and aril: psychic disturbances by 5 g of seed, atropine-like action by 9 teaspoons of seed powder, abortion by higher doses. The EO contains the mutagenic and animal carcinogenic compound safrole. However, the use to correct smell or taste is permitted (AEH). On overdose, there may be hallucination and emesis; there may be frightening visions, a sensation of loss of limbs and a terrifying fear of impending death. Indeed, death has been reported from overdose (LRNP, September 1987).</data></node>
<node id="ind:agoraphobia"><data key="n_label">Agoraphobia</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="ind:hypercalcemia"><data key="n_label">Hypercalcemia</data><data key="n_type">Indicación</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Indications</data></node>
<node id="hierba:strychnos-nux-vomica-l-nux-vomica"><data key="n_label">Nux Vomica</data><data key="n_type">Hierba</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">NUX VOMICA</data><data key="n_page">531</data><data key="n_scientific">Strychnos nux-vomica L.</data><data key="n_safety">X</data><data key="n_safety_raw">X</data><data key="n_dosages">APA recommends only homeopathic dilutions (APA); 0.5–3 g powdered seed (KAP); 0.5–2 ml seed tincture (PNC); 0.5–1 g extract (KAP); 0.5–1 ml tincture (KAP); 5 ml nux vomica elixir (PNC); 0.05–0.2 ml nux vomica liquid extract (PNC).</data><data key="n_downsides">Not covered (AHP); Commission E reports the seed contains the toxic alkaloid strychnine (AEH). Poisoning can generate anxiety, backache, enhanced reflexes, equilibrium disorders, heightened sense perception, pain, and stiff neck, then convulsions, dyspnea, and twitching. Lethal dose ca. 50 mg strychnine (1–2 g seed) (PH2).</data></node>
<node id="act:cholinolytic"><data key="n_label">Cholinolytic</data><data key="n_type">Actividad</data><data key="n_description">Citada en 1 monografías</data><data key="n_source">Duke JA et al. Handbook of Medicinal Herbs, 2nd ed. CRC Press, 2002</data><data key="n_chapter">Catalog of Herbs (A to Z)</data><data key="n_section">Activities</data></node>
